/* Question bank PA test (English convenience translation), Wft Non-life Insurance (Commercial). Vorm: [vraag, [[antwoord, uitleg] x4], toetsterm CDFD]; het EERSTE antwoord is steeds het juiste.
   Gegenereerd met tools/vragen-js.py. */
(window.PA_VRAGEN = window.PA_VRAGEN || {})["schade-zakelijk"] = [
["Which sequence fits a systematic commercial risk inventory?", [["Identify the risks, analyse them for likelihood and impact, and then choose a measure.", "You first need to know which risks exist and how serious they are; only then can you choose between avoiding, reducing, transferring or retaining them."], ["First set the premium budget and then see which risks fit within it.", "The budget is a constraint, but the starting point is the company's risk profile; otherwise major risks may remain unexamined."], ["Insure every conceivable risk and review after a loss which covers turned out to be unnecessary.", "That is not an inventory but the opposite of one; it leads to unnecessary premium and may still leave gaps in cover."], ["Only work out the risks the business owner mentions in the intake meeting.", "The adviser must also flag risks the business owner does not see; clients cannot see their own blind spots."]], "1c.1"],
["Fatima el Amrani runs a lunchroom chain with four branches and twenty employees in Utrecht. Her large street-front windows break on average three times a year, usually through vandalism or a ball from children playing. Each loss costs a few hundred euros and is currently paid in full by the glass insurance. The insurer has therefore raised the premium sharply, and Fatima asks her adviser what would be sensible. Which approach is the most obvious?", [["Retain these small, predictable losses largely herself, for example through a higher excess / deductible.", "Fatima pays back frequent small losses through the premium anyway, plus the insurer's costs; retaining them is then often more efficient."], ["Keep insuring the glass in full without an excess / deductible, because the premium is then always the lowest.", "With frequent small losses the client pays those losses back in the premium plus costs and margin; that does not produce a low premium."], ["Close the branches with the most glass damage, because the risk is then avoided entirely.", "Avoidance is entirely disproportionate here; the business would give up turnover for a limited and manageable risk."], ["Place the glass risk directly with a reinsurer who handles small losses more cheaply.", "A reinsurer insures insurers and does not write policies with individual business owners; that is not an option for Fatima."]], "1e.2"],
["Which risk is particularly suitable for transfer through insurance?", [["A risk with a small likelihood but a very large loss that threatens continuity.", "The business cannot bear this kind of risk itself, while the low likelihood keeps the premium affordable; that is what insurance is for."], ["A risk with a high likelihood and a small loss that the business can easily absorb.", "Such losses are predictable and are paid by the business owner through the premium anyway; retaining them is more obvious."], ["A loss that is certain to occur, such as normal wear and tear of machinery.", "Insurance requires uncertainty; an event that is certain to happen is not an insurable risk but a cost item."], ["A business risk, such as declining demand for the company's products.", "The general business risk of disappointing turnover is not insurable; it is part of doing business."]], "3b.1"],
["During a risk inventory, an adviser uses a matrix of likelihood and impact. How is a risk with both a high likelihood and a high impact best approached?", [["Mainly with measures that reduce likelihood or impact, or by avoiding the risk.", "A risk that strikes both often and severely is usually not insurable, or only at a very high premium; prevention or avoidance then comes first."], ["Solely by retaining the risk and building up a reserve for it.", "Retention suits small, predictable losses; with a high impact it can threaten the continuity of the business."], ["By accepting the risk because it cannot be controlled anyway.", "Precisely this kind of risk calls for active management; accepting it without measures is irresponsible."], ["By insuring it with the lowest possible excess / deductible and a generous sum insured.", "An insurer will not accept a risk with a high likelihood and high impact, or only at a very high premium; insuring is not the first step here."]], "2d.2"],
["What is the main purpose of a company visit during the risk inventory of a manufacturing company?", [["To see how the business actually works: type of construction, prevention and dependencies.", "On site, the adviser sees risks such as fire compartmentation, storage of hazardous substances and critical machines, which is needed for suitable advice."], ["To determine which insurer charges the lowest premium for this company in the market.", "A premium comparison can be done without a visit; the visit serves to understand the risk itself."], ["To check whether the company's tax and financial records are in order for the year.", "That is not a task of the non-life insurance adviser; the risk inventory is about insurable risks."], ["To agree in advance how high a future claim payment will be if a loss should occur.", "The payment depends on the size of a future loss and on the policy; this cannot be determined during a company visit."]], "2b.1"],
["What is understood by fire in non-life insurance according to the customary definition?", [["A fire outside a hearth, with flames that can spread under their own power.", "This is the core of the standard definition used by insurers; it concerns a fire that propagates independently."], ["Any damage caused by heat or fire, including scorching, singeing, melting and charring.", "Scorching, singeing, melting, charring and smouldering without flames are specifically not regarded as fire."], ["Any fire that causes damage, even if it remains within a hearth or stove.", "A fire in a hearth is an intended fire and not a fire in this sense; there must be a fire outside a hearth."], ["Any event in which smoke is produced, even without flames.", "Smoke alone is not fire; smoke and soot damage is often covered, but as a separate event."]], "1c.8"],
["In a restaurant, an overly hot iron scorches a tablecloth without any flames arising. Is this fire damage within the meaning of fire insurance?", [["No, scorching without flames is not covered by the concept of fire.", "Without flames that can spread under their own power there is no fire; scorching damage falls outside it."], ["Yes, because the damage was caused by heat.", "Heat alone is not sufficient; the definition requires fire with flames outside a hearth."], ["Yes, provided the damage is higher than the excess / deductible.", "The excess / deductible only determines how much is paid out, not whether an event counts as fire."], ["No, because a restaurant with an open kitchen has an increased fire risk.", "The nature of the business affects premium and acceptance, but not the question of whether scorching is fire."]], "1e.1"],
["Bram Hoekstra has a bicycle shop with a workshop in a rented building in Zwolle, with six employees. Last year he had a new glass front, a floor and a fixed counter installed at his own expense, together worth more than €60,000. The landlord has insured the building itself with a buildings policy. Bram has a contents insurance for his bicycles and tools. How can Bram best insure his investment in the building?", [["Through tenant's interest cover, usually insured as an addition to his contents or business assets policy.", "The improvements are at Bram's expense as tenant; tenant's interest cover is intended precisely for this risk."], ["Not separately, because alterations made by a tenant are always automatically insured under the landlord's buildings policy.", "That cannot be taken for granted; the landlord insures his own interest, so Bram may turn out to be uninsured."], ["Through his business liability insurance, because the renovation concerns someone else's building.", "The AVB covers liability towards others, not damage to the insured's own investments."], ["Through a business interruption insurance, which also reimburses the costs of a new glass front after a fire.", "A business interruption insurance covers the loss of gross profit, not the material damage to the fit-out."]], "1c.3"],
["At what value is a commercial building normally insured under a buildings insurance if it is rebuilt after a loss?", [["The rebuilding value.", "The rebuilding value is the amount needed to rebuild the building, in the same form and for the same use, immediately after the loss."], ["The WOZ value.", "The WOZ value (municipal property valuation) is a tax value including land and does not match the cost of rebuilding."], ["The market value including land.", "Land is not lost in a fire and the market value follows the property market, not construction costs."], ["The book value according to the annual accounts.", "After depreciation the book value is often much lower than the rebuilding costs and would lead to a shortfall."]], "1e.3"],
["At what value is trade stock (inventory) normally insured under a goods insurance?", [["The replacement value: what it costs to buy or make the goods again.", "The insurance compensates the material loss; the lost profit margin falls under a business interruption insurance."], ["The selling price, including the profit margin that would have been made on the goods.", "The insurance would then also compensate profit that was never made; that loss belongs under business interruption."], ["The tax book value of the stock after depreciation.", "Stock is not depreciated in that way and the book value says little about the cost of replacement."], ["The average value of the stock over the past year.", "An average says nothing about the value at the time of the loss and may lead to underinsurance."]], "1h.1"],
["Garden centre De Groene Hof in Boskoop has fifteen employees and strongly seasonal turnover. In March and April there are more than €400,000 of plants and garden products in stock, in December less than €100,000. Owner Kees Verbeek does not want to pay premium all year round on the peak amount. At the same time, he does not want to face underinsurance in the event of a fire in spring. Which solution fits best?", [["A seasonal or peak cover, or insuring the stock on a declaration basis.", "The sum insured then follows the actual stock and the premium is aligned with the value during the year."], ["Insuring the stock at the annual average, so that high and low months cancel each other out.", "In the peak months the value is then well above the sum insured, so underinsurance may be applied."], ["Insuring at the lowest stock level and increasing the cover for the difference with the peak after a loss.", "After a loss, the cover cannot be increased retroactively; the underinsurance is then already a fact."], ["Having the stock insured under the buildings policy of the greenhouses and the shop premises.", "A buildings policy covers the building itself, not movable property such as the stock lying in it."]], "1e.6"],
["Which of the following forms belongs to the six forms of molest in the customary standard definition in non-life policies?", [["Civil disturbances.", "Molest comprises armed conflict, civil war, insurrection, civil disturbances, riots and mutiny."], ["Terrorism.", "Terrorism is not a form of molest; a separate clause applies to terrorism damage."], ["Flooding due to a dyke breach.", "Flooding is a natural hazard and not molest; it has its own exclusion or cover."], ["A large-scale cyber attack.", "A cyber attack is none of the six forms of molest; cyber is dealt with separately in policies."]], "1c.8"],
["How is terrorism damage usually arranged in Dutch non-life insurance?", [["Covered through a terrorism clause, with the payment possibly limited in the event of a large loss.", "Insurers have jointly reinsured this risk with a reinsurer set up for this purpose; in a major disaster the payment may be proportionally limited."], ["Fully excluded on all commercial policies, like molest, without any exception being possible.", "Terrorism is in fact insured within the limits of the clause; a full exclusion is not the standard."], ["Covered by the government under the Compensation for Damage in Disasters Act (WTS).", "The WTS is intended for disaster damage that is not reasonably insurable and does not replace terrorism cover."], ["Covered without limit, without any reservation as to the size of the payment to be made.", "The terrorism clause does contain a reservation: in the event of a large loss burden a payment percentage may be set."]], "1e.2"],
["Jeweller Van Dalen in Haarlem has two employees and a contents and stock insurance. The policy states as a condition that the burglar alarm with a connection to an alarm receiving centre must be switched on outside opening hours. On a Saturday evening the last employee forgets to switch on the alarm. That night there is a burglary and jewellery worth €85,000 is stolen. What is correct?", [["The insurer can rely on the prevention condition and refuse or limit the payment.", "Prevention conditions are contractual agreements; whether the reliance succeeds depends on the wording and reasonableness, but the risk of refusal is real."], ["There are no consequences, because prevention requirements in a policy are merely non-binding recommendations.", "Prevention requirements in the policy are binding conditions and not non-binding advice."], ["The insurer must pay in full, because burglary is a covered event under this policy.", "That burglary is covered does not mean the cover also applies if the policy conditions have not been met."], ["The insurance is automatically terminated retroactively to the start date.", "The consequence is not automatic termination with retroactive effect; it concerns the cover for this loss."]], "1i.2"],
["What is usually understood by gross profit in a business interruption insurance?", [["Turnover minus variable costs, i.e. fixed costs plus net profit.", "In a stoppage the variable costs fall away; the fixed costs continue and the profit is lost. That sum is the insured interest."], ["Turnover minus all costs, including the company's fixed costs.", "That is net profit; the fixed costs that continue during a stoppage would then fall outside the cover."], ["The company's total turnover, including VAT.", "Turnover also includes variable costs that are not incurred during a stoppage; compensating them would enrich the business owner."], ["Net profit plus corporate income tax.", "That is profit before tax and ignores the continuing fixed costs."]], "1e.18"],
["What is the indemnity period in a business interruption insurance?", [["The period after the date of loss over which the insurer compensates the loss of gross profit.", "Within this period the business must be back at its former level; loss after that falls outside the cover."], ["The period within which the insurer must pay the determined loss amount after a claim is reported.", "That is a payment period; the indemnity period concerns the period over which the loss is compensated."], ["The contract term for which the insurance has been taken out.", "The policy term is separate from the period over which business interruption is compensated after a loss."], ["The waiting period during which the business loss remains at the business owner's expense.", "That is an excess / deductible in time; the indemnity period is the period of compensation."]], "1e.20"],
["Machine factory Brouwer in Hengelo, with forty employees, makes components for the food industry. In a fire the production hall and three purpose-built milling machines are lost. The supplier has a delivery time of one year for new machines, and rebuilding the hall also takes months. The business interruption policy has an indemnity period of 52 weeks, while full recovery is expected to take eighteen months. What is the consequence?", [["The loss of gross profit after the 52 weeks is not compensated, even though the interruption continues.", "The indemnity period is too short for the recovery time; delivery times and rebuilding duration should have led to a longer period when advice was given."], ["The insurer automatically extends the indemnity period until the business is fully producing again.", "The indemnity period is a contractual limit; automatic extension is not standard."], ["The indemnity period only starts to run when the new milling machines have been delivered by the supplier.", "The period normally starts on the date of loss, not on the moment the machines are back."], ["Within the 52 weeks the insurer still compensates the expected loss over all eighteen months.", "The insurer only compensates the loss suffered within the indemnity period."]], "1e.21"],
["What is usually a condition for cover under a traditional business interruption insurance?", [["The stoppage is the result of material damage caused by a covered event, such as fire.", "Business interruption is linked to material damage covered under the policy; without that cause there is no cover."], ["Any decline in turnover the business suffers, even if caused by lower demand.", "Declining demand is business risk and is not covered by a business interruption insurance."], ["The business owner has no staff who have to continue to be paid during the stoppage.", "Staff costs are often part of the continuing fixed costs that are compensated."], ["The stoppage lasts longer than the indemnity period.", "The indemnity period is the maximum period of compensation, not a minimum duration of the stoppage."]], "1e.17"],
["Bakery Van der Wal in Leeuwarden, with 25 employees, supplies bread to supermarkets in Friesland every day. All the flour comes from one specialist mill nearby, because the recipes are tuned to that flour. One night the mill burns down completely. The bakery itself has no damage, but cannot bake in the usual way for weeks and loses turnover. Which cover could have absorbed this loss?", [["A business interruption insurance with a supplier dependency cover.", "This extension compensates the loss of gross profit when the stoppage is caused by damage at a business on which the insured depends."], ["A tenant's interest cover on the bakery's contents policy.", "Tenant's interest covers improvements by a tenant to a rented building, not the failure of a supplier."], ["The bakery's own business liability insurance.", "The AVB covers liability towards third parties, not own loss of turnover due to damage at a supplier."], ["A traditional business interruption insurance without additional extensions.", "A traditional business interruption policy requires material damage at the insured itself; that did not occur here."]], "1e.17"],
["Accountancy firm Mulder & Partners in Amersfoort has thirty employees and works for more than five hundred SME clients. In the busy period before tax returns are due, the firm cannot afford days of standstill, because clients would then leave. Turnover hardly falls after a fire if the firm can quickly continue working elsewhere. The management mainly wants to cover the costs of temporary accommodation, extra equipment and overtime. Which cover fits best?", [["An additional expenses insurance, also called extra-cost insurance.", "It compensates the extra costs needed to continue operations as far as possible after a loss."], ["A buildings insurance of the office premises on a rebuilding value basis.", "It covers the repair of the building, not the costs of working elsewhere temporarily."], ["A commercial legal expenses insurance with cover for rental disputes.", "Legal expenses insurance helps with legal disputes and does not compensate temporary accommodation or overtime."], ["A professional liability insurance for the accountants' errors.", "It covers clients' claims arising from professional errors, not the firm's own extra costs after a fire."]], "1e.24"],
["Why is it important that the insured gross profit under a business interruption insurance is updated regularly?", [["Because if the insured gross profit is too low, underinsurance may apply.", "If the business grows, the old sum no longer covers the loss in full and the payment may be proportionally reduced."], ["Because the indemnity period automatically becomes shorter if the declaration is outdated.", "The indemnity period is a fixed agreement and does not depend on the amount of gross profit declared."], ["Because the insurer automatically terminates the policy if the declaration is outdated.", "An outdated declaration does not lead to termination; the risk lies in a payment that is too low."], ["Because the amount of gross profit determines how many waiting days apply.", "The waiting period is a separate agreement in the policy and is unrelated to the insured gross profit."]], "1i.7"],
["Which loss is usually covered under a business liability insurance (AVB)?", [["Personal injury and property damage of third parties, including resulting consequential loss.", "The AVB is aimed at injury and damage to property of third parties, including the loss resulting from them."], ["Pure financial loss of clients caused by incorrect or incomplete advice given by the company.", "Loss without injury or property damage generally falls outside the AVB; professional liability insurance is intended for that."], ["Damage to the insured company's own contents and stock.", "Own loss falls under a contents or goods insurance; the AVB covers loss suffered by third parties."], ["Contractual penalties that the company owes to its customers under the agreements made.", "Penalties and obligations arising solely from a contract are generally excluded under an AVB."]], "1c.17"],
["Installation company Jansen Techniek in Tilburg employs twelve fitters. A fitter replaces a part of a central heating boiler at a customer's premises and tightens a connection too far, so the boiler is damaged beyond repair. The customer demands the cost of a new boiler. The company has a standard business liability insurance (AVB). Why is the damage to the boiler itself often not covered, or only to a limited extent, under that AVB?", [["Because of the care, custody and control clause for items the insured is working on.", "Damage to items the insured is working on is excluded or only limitedly covered in most policies; insurers do not want to cover the quality risk of the insured's own work."], ["Because installation companies are not insurable for liability, given their high risk profile.", "Installers can perfectly well take out an AVB; this concerns a specific exclusion."], ["Because it concerns personal injury, which falls outside the cover of a standard AVB policy.", "This concerns property damage; personal injury is in fact a core cover under an AVB."], ["Because the AVB only covers loss arising outside the work carried out by the company itself.", "The AVB in fact covers liability arising from the conduct of the business, so also during the work."]], "1e.2"],
["Which form of liability applies under the Dutch Civil Code (BW) to the producer of a defective product?", [["Strict liability: the producer's fault does not have to be shown.", "The product liability regime in the BW places liability on the producer regardless of blame."], ["Fault-based liability: the injured party must show culpable conduct by the producer.", "The fault requirement has in fact been dropped for product liability in order to protect the injured party."], ["Exclusively contractual liability towards the direct buyer of the product.", "The regime also applies towards injured parties without a contract with the producer, such as users and bystanders."], ["Liability only if the producer acted intentionally.", "Intent is not required; the producer is liable for a defective product even without any fault."]], "1g.3"],
["Meat products producer Smit in Oss, with 120 employees, supplies supermarket chains in the Benelux. An internal check shows a batch of sausage to be contaminated with listeria. Nobody has fallen ill yet, but the company decides to recall all products from the shops and to announce this publicly. The costs of recalling, destroying and communicating run into hundreds of thousands of euros. Are these recall costs covered under a standard AVB?", [["Usually not; a separate product recall insurance is needed for that.", "Standard AVB policies generally exclude recall costs; this risk is insured separately."], ["Yes, recall costs are always covered under an AVB through the product liability cover.", "Product liability covers loss suffered by third parties; the costs of recalling products are mostly excluded."], ["Yes, but only if a regulator or court has ordered the recall.", "An order does not make recall costs covered if the policy excludes them."], ["No, because these costs are covered under the business interruption insurance as standard.", "Business interruption usually requires material damage caused by a covered event; recall costs are not a standard cover there."]], "1c.17"],
["Within which periods can an injured party hold a producer liable under the product liability regime in the Dutch Civil Code (BW)?", [["Limitation three years after becoming aware of the damage, defect and producer; expiry ten years after circulation.", "This combination of limitation and expiry periods comes from the European Product Liability Directive and is included in the BW."], ["Limitation of five years after becoming aware of the damage and the liable party; expiry twenty years after the damage-causing event.", "These are the general periods for compensation of damage; shorter periods apply to product liability."], ["Limitation of one year after becoming aware of the damage and the producer; expiry five years after purchase of the product concerned.", "These periods are too short and do not match the statutory regime."], ["Limitation of two years after the damage arose; there is no expiry period for products.", "An expiry period does exist, so that the producer has certainty after a certain time."]], "1g.3"],
["An employee is injured at work. What must the employer in principle show under the Dutch Civil Code (BW) in order not to be liable?", [["That it met its duty of care, or that the employee acted intentionally or with deliberate recklessness.", "The burden of proof lies with the employer; slight carelessness on the part of the employee does not relieve it of liability."], ["Nothing; the injured employee must prove that the employer culpably failed to meet its obligations.", "In employer's liability the burden of proof is reversed; the employer must show that it has done enough."], ["That the employee was slightly careless in carrying out the work.", "Ordinary carelessness of the employee is for the employer's account; only intent or deliberate recklessness counts."], ["That it has taken out a suitable business liability insurance and has paid the premium on time.", "Insurance says nothing about the liability itself; it only finances the consequences."]], "1i.24"],
["Construction company Kuipers in Apeldoorn builds homes with thirty permanent employees and hires freelancers in busy periods. A hired carpenter falls from a scaffold that the construction company built itself and on which a guard rail was missing. He breaks his pelvis and cannot work for months. He holds Kuipers liable for his loss of income and costs. What is correct?", [["Kuipers can, like an employer, be liable for the safety of the freelancer's workplace.", "The BW extends employer's liability to anyone who has work carried out in his business by non-employees; the AVB must then also cover this risk."], ["Kuipers is never liable, because a freelancer as a business owner always bears his own accident risk.", "That is too absolute; the duty of care for a safe workplace can also apply towards hired workers."], ["Kuipers is only liable if the carpenter turns out afterwards to be a disguised employee.", "Even without an employment contract, the principal can be liable under the extended employer's liability."], ["Only the manufacturer of the scaffold components can be held liable for this loss.", "A product defect is not at issue here; the guard rail was missing when the construction company itself erected the scaffold."]], "1i.24"],
["Sanne de Boer is an account manager at wholesaler Verhoef in Breda, a company with sixty employees. She visits customers in her own car and receives a mileage allowance. On the way to a customer she is forced off the road by a driver who remains unknown, and suffers injury and damage to her car. Verhoef has not arranged accident or passenger insurance for such journeys. On what ground can Verhoef nevertheless be liable for her loss?", [["On the ground of good employership, because the employer must provide proper insurance.", "According to established case law, the employer must provide suitable insurance for employees who take part in traffic for their work; if it is missing, the employer may have to bear the loss itself."], ["On the ground of the WAM policy of the company cars, which also insures employees' own cars.", "A WAM policy only covers the vehicles insured under it and moreover does not compensate the driver's own loss."], ["On the ground of product liability, because the car was used for Verhoef's work.", "Product liability concerns defective products and has no relevance here."], ["Not at all, because for a business journey in her own car the employer is never liable for her loss.", "Precisely for business journeys the employer can be liable on the ground of good employership, even in her own car."]], "1i.24"],
["Which loss does a professional liability insurance typically cover that is usually outside the cover of an AVB?", [["Pure financial loss of a client caused by a professional error.", "Professionals such as advisers, accountants and architects mainly cause financial loss without injury or property damage; this insurance is intended for that."], ["Injury to a visitor who slips on a wet floor at the office.", "That is personal injury of a third party and falls under the AVB."], ["Damage to the firm's own office contents caused by a burst water pipe.", "Own loss falls under a contents insurance, not under a liability insurance."], ["Damage to the firm's own company car after a collision on the way to a client.", "Damage to one's own vehicle falls under the comprehensive cover of the car insurance."]], "1c.17"],
["A professional liability insurance has been taken out on a claims-made basis. What does that mean?", [["Claims made and reported during the policy term are covered, within the run-in arrangement.", "The moment of the claim is decisive, not the moment of the error; therefore run-in and run-off arrangements are important when switching or stopping."], ["Cover applies to errors during the policy term, even if the claim is only brought years after termination.", "That describes a cover based on the moment of the event; under claims-made the moment of the claim is decisive."], ["The excess / deductible applies per claim instead of per event.", "Claims-made concerns the time limitation of the cover, not the application of the excess / deductible."], ["The insurer only pays if the client starts court proceedings.", "A written notice of liability outside court also counts as a claim; proceedings are not required."]], "1c.19"],
["Cleaning company Van Gils in The Hague has 85 employees who clean offices. While vacuuming at a law firm, an employee accidentally knocks a bronze statue off its plinth. The statue is damaged and restoration costs €12,000. The law firm wants to know whom it can hold liable. Who is in principle liable towards the law firm?", [["The cleaning company as employer, which is liable for errors of its employees.", "An employer is liable for errors that employees make in the course of their work; the law firm can hold Van Gils liable."], ["Solely the employee who personally knocked the statue off the plinth.", "The firm can hold the employer liable; moreover, employees are protected against recourse by the employer, except in the case of intent or deliberate recklessness."], ["Nobody, because it was an accident without any intent on the part of the employee.", "A wrongful act does not require intent; a careless act during work can be sufficient."], ["The law firm itself, because the statue was not securely fastened to the plinth.", "Contributory fault can at most play a role in the division of the loss, but does not in principle make the injured party itself liable."]], "1i.24"],
["Who is usually the policyholder and who are the insured persons in a directors' and officers' liability insurance (D&O)?", [["The company is the policyholder; directors and supervisors are the insured persons.", "The legal entity takes out and pays for the policy, but it covers the personal liability of the directors and supervisors."], ["Each director is the policyholder himself and the company is the insured party.", "The D&O policy in fact covers the personal liability of directors, not primarily the company."], ["The shareholders are insured for the loss on their investment in the company concerned.", "Shareholders are often the very party that holds directors liable; their investment risk is not insured."], ["All employees are insured for the errors they make in their work for the company.", "D&O is aimed at directors and supervisors; employees are in principle not covered by it."]], "1c.17"],
["A trustee in bankruptcy holds the directors of a bankrupt private limited company (bv) liable because the annual accounts were filed late in the three years before the bankruptcy. What is the consequence?", [["Improper management is established and is presumed to be an important cause of the bankruptcy.", "Breach of the publication duty gives established improper management and a rebuttable presumption of causation."], ["There are no consequences, because filing late is merely an administrative error.", "The law in fact attaches far-reaching consequences in bankruptcy to non-compliance with the publication duty (an unimportant omission aside)."], ["The director receives only an administrative fine and is not civilly liable.", "This concerns civil liability towards the estate, not a fine."], ["The director is jointly and severally liable for the entire shortfall and can no longer provide any counter-evidence.", "The director can rebut the presumption that improper management is an important cause of the bankruptcy."]], "1g.3"],
["When, according to established case law, is a director internally liable towards the company for improper performance of duties?", [["When a serious reproach can be made against the director.", "The Dutch Supreme Court (Hoge Raad) sets the bar high to give directors room for entrepreneurship; an ordinary mistake is not enough."], ["For every mistake that leads to loss.", "Managing would then become unworkable; the standard is a serious reproach."], ["Only after a criminal conviction.", "Civil liability does not require a criminal conviction."], ["Only if all shareholders unanimously agree to it.", "Liability follows from the law and case law, not from the agreement of shareholders."]], "1g.3"],
["Which costs form a core element of D&O insurance, even if the claim ultimately proves unfounded?", [["The costs of the director's defence and legal assistance.", "In claims against directors, defence costs mount up quickly; D&O pays them even if the director is ultimately not liable."], ["The administrative fines imposed on the company.", "Fines are generally not insurable, or only to a limited extent, and here concern the company itself."], ["The company's loss of turnover caused by the claim.", "The company's business loss falls outside D&O, which covers the personal liability of directors."], ["Continued payment of the director's salary during the proceedings.", "Loss of the director's income is not part of D&O cover."]], "1c.4"],
["Which exclusion is usual in a D&O policy?", [["Loss caused by intent or fraud of the director, once established.", "Knowingly obtaining an unlawful advantage is also often excluded; until intent or fraud is established by judgment or admission, defence costs are often still advanced."], ["Any claim that a trustee brings against directors after the bankruptcy.", "Claims by trustees are in fact an important risk for which D&O is intended."], ["Claims based on improper performance of duties by the board.", "Improper performance of duties is precisely the core of directors' liability and therefore of the cover."], ["All costs of legal defence incurred by a director.", "Defence costs are a core cover of D&O, not an exclusion."]], "1e.2"],
["Which two elements form the core of a Construction All Risks insurance (CAR)?", [["Damage to the works in progress and liability towards third parties.", "The CAR covers the construction project itself and the liability arising from the building activities, supplemented with optional sections."], ["Loss of income of the contractor and sickness absence costs of its staff during construction.", "Those are income and absence risks that are covered by other insurances."], ["The client's buildings after completion, and glass breakage.", "After completion the building is a buildings risk of the owner; the CAR concerns the construction phase."], ["The transport of building materials and the carrier's CMR liability.", "Transport risks and carrier's liability fall under transport insurances."]], "1c.4"],
["Who are usually jointly insured under a project-specific CAR insurance?", [["The client, main contractor and subcontractors, often also designers.", "A joint insurance prevents discussion and recourse between construction partners; exactly who is insured is stated in the policy."], ["Only the client, because it has the project carried out.", "The CAR is in fact intended to bring all parties involved in construction under one policy."], ["Only the main contractor; subcontractors are never co-insured.", "Subcontractors are generally co-insured on a project CAR."], ["Only the future tenants and users of the building.", "Future users have no role in the construction phase and are not insured parties under the CAR."]], "1c.3"],
["Construction consortium Zijlstra-Bakker is building a four-storey car park in Groningen for the municipality. The project is insured under a project-specific CAR policy. Because of a calculation error by the structural engineer, the reinforcement of a concrete floor has been made too light. During construction that floor collapses, also damaging the installations already fitted below it. How does a CAR policy usually deal with this?", [["Repair of the defective part itself is often excluded; consequential damage is usually covered.", "The CAR is not a quality guarantee, but it does protect against the damage a defect causes elsewhere in the works, such as to the installations."], ["All costs, including recalculating and re-pouring the floor, are fully covered by the policy.", "Repairing the defect itself is usually outside the cover; otherwise the CAR would become a quality guarantee."], ["Nothing is covered, because the cause of the loss is a design or calculation error by the engineer.", "A design error does not exclude all cover; the consequential damage to other parts is generally covered."], ["Only the professional liability insurance of the structural engineer can pay anything for this.", "The engineer's professional liability insurance may play a role in recourse, but the CAR itself usually compensates the consequential damage."]], "1e.2"],
["According to the UAV 2012, at whose risk is the work in principle until the moment of completion?", [["At the contractor's risk.", "Until completion the contractor in principle bears the risk of damage to the works; a CAR insurance is aligned with this."], ["At the client's risk.", "The risk in principle passes only at completion; until then it lies with the contractor, subject to exceptions."], ["At the joint risk of contractor and client, each for half.", "The UAV has no standard split in half; the main rule places the risk on the contractor."], ["At the architect's risk.", "The architect is a designer and not a party that bears the risk of the works in progress."]], "1c.18"],
["To which transport does the CMR Convention apply?", [["Paid international carriage of goods by road between two countries, at least one of which is a CMR country.", "This is the core of the scope of the convention; domestic carriage falls outside it."], ["All paid carriage of goods by road, even if it takes place entirely within the Netherlands or the Benelux.", "Domestic road carriage does not fall under the CMR but under national law."], ["International carriage of goods by sea between ports of different countries.", "Sea carriage has its own conventions and rules; the CMR concerns road carriage."], ["Exclusively international removals and the carriage of human remains.", "These forms of carriage are in fact excluded from the CMR."]], "1c.13"],
["Up to what amount is the road carrier's liability under the CMR in principle limited for loss of or damage to the cargo?", [["8.33 units of account (SDR) per kilogram of gross weight short.", "This limit applies unless a higher value or a special interest has been declared, or there is intent or fault equivalent to it."], ["The full actual value of the goods, without any limit.", "The CMR in fact has a limit per kilogram; as a result the cargo interest may be left with a shortfall."], ["A fixed amount per consignment, regardless of weight.", "The limit is calculated per kilogram of gross weight, not per consignment."], ["2 units of account (SDR) per kilogram of gross weight short.", "A limit of that order is found in sea carriage; the CMR uses 8.33 SDR per kilogram."]], "1c.15"],
["Cheese exporter Hoogendoorn in Gouda sends lorries of cheese to customers in Italy and Spain every week. The company has twenty employees and an average cargo value of €80,000 per load. The director considers a transport insurance unnecessary, because the hired carrier has a CMR insurance. He asks his adviser whether that is right. What is the most important counter-argument?", [["The CMR insurance only covers the carrier's limited liability, not the cargo itself.", "For loss for which the carrier is not liable, or above the per-kilo limit, the exporter receives nothing; a goods transport insurance covers its own interest."], ["A carrier's CMR insurance applies only to road carriage within the Netherlands.", "The CMR insurance in fact concerns international road carriage; the problem lies in the limited liability."], ["The CMR insurance is only valid if the exporter is also a policyholder on that policy.", "The carrier is the policyholder; therefore the policy only covers its liability."], ["The Italian and Spanish recipients are legally required to insure the cargo themselves.", "There is no general legal obligation for the recipient; who insures follows from the agreements, such as the Incoterms."]], "1c.13"],
["Within what period does a claim under the CMR usually become time-barred?", [["One year; three years in the case of intent or equivalent fault.", "The CMR has a short limitation period; cargo interests and their insurers must therefore act quickly."], ["Three years; five years in the case of intent or equivalent fault.", "These periods are longer than the convention provides."], ["Six months; one year in the case of intent or equivalent fault.", "The limitation period is shorter than for ordinary claims, but not as short as six months."], ["Five years; twenty years in the case of intent or equivalent fault.", "This resembles the general periods for compensation of damage in the BW, not the CMR."]], "1c.13"],
["Pump manufacturer Hylkema in Kampen sells a large installation to a water company in Vietnam. The contract is concluded on the basis of the Incoterm CIF, with a port in Vietnam as destination. The installation is loaded on board a container ship in Rotterdam. The sales manager asks who has to arrange the transport insurance. Who has to do so under this Incoterm?", [["Hylkema as seller, for the benefit of the buyer, with at least minimum cover.", "Under CIF (Cost, Insurance and Freight) the seller arranges freight and insurance; the risk already passes on loading on board."], ["The water company as buyer, because the risk already passes to it on loading in Rotterdam.", "The risk does indeed pass early, but under CIF the seller is obliged to arrange the insurance."], ["The shipping line, as an integral part of the contract of carriage with Hylkema.", "The carrier at most insures its own liability, not the goods for the benefit of buyer or seller."], ["Nobody, because under CIF insurance is not needed for sea carriage.", "The 'I' in CIF in fact stands for the seller's obligation to insure."]], "1c.16"],
["What is a characteristic of a fleet insurance compared with separate car policies?", [["All vehicles are on one policy; the premium often follows the claims experience of the whole.", "Instead of a bonus-malus scale per car, the insurer looks at the loss burden of the fleet as a whole."], ["The statutory WAM insurance obligation does not apply to vehicles on a fleet policy.", "The WAM obligation applies to every motor vehicle taking part in traffic, also on a fleet policy."], ["A fleet policy covers only comprehensive (own damage) cover.", "A fleet policy in any case includes the compulsory WAM cover, with comprehensive cover possibly in addition."], ["A fleet policy is only possible for private cars that employees use for business journeys.", "The fleet policy is in fact intended for a company's business vehicles."]], "1c.12"],
["Plumbing company Bakker & Zn in Alkmaar has eight vans on a fleet policy. After work a fitter drinks a few beers and then drives the van home. He hits a cyclist, who ends up in hospital with a broken leg. The policy excludes loss that occurs while the driver is driving under the influence. Can the WAM insurer invoke against the cyclist that the policy excludes driving under the influence?", [["No; the cyclist has a direct right against the WAM insurer, which can then seek recourse.", "The WAM protects the victim: the insurer cannot invoke policy exclusions against the injured party, but after paying out can seek recourse against the insured."], ["Yes; the cyclist must then recover his loss personally from the fitter or the company.", "The protection of road accident victims would then depend on the driver's behaviour, which the WAM in fact prevents."], ["Yes; in that case the cyclist must turn to the Motor Traffic Guarantee Fund (Waarborgfonds Motorverkeer).", "The Guarantee Fund steps in where there is no insurance or the person who caused the loss is unknown, not in the case of a policy exclusion."], ["No, and the insurer cannot recover the amount paid out from anyone afterwards either.", "The insurer pays the injured party but, in the case of an exclusion, can seek recourse against the insured."]], "1g.1"],
["Why is a passenger damage insurance (SVI) relevant for a fleet in addition to WAM cover?", [["The WAM does not cover the driver; the SVI does, regardless of fault.", "For employers this aligns with the duty as a good employer to provide proper insurance for employees in traffic."], ["The SVI is legally compulsory for every company vehicle on the road.", "Only the WAM is legally compulsory; the SVI is a supplementary, voluntary cover."], ["The SVI covers damage to the cargo carried in vans.", "Cargo falls under a goods or transport insurance, not under a passenger insurance."], ["The SVI replaces the comprehensive cover of the vehicles in the fleet.", "Comprehensive cover covers damage to the vehicle itself; the SVI covers loss of persons in the vehicle."]], "1c.4"],
["A company with a fleet policy buys three new vans during the policy year. What does a fleet policy often provide about this?", [["They are automatically co-insured, provided they are reported in time before settlement.", "This prevents new vehicles from going on the road uninsured; the premium is settled afterwards."], ["They are only covered after a separate policy has been taken out for each of the vehicles.", "Automatic co-insurance is in fact an advantage of a fleet policy."], ["They can only be added to the policy at the start of the next contract year of the policy.", "Adding during the term is usual; otherwise vehicles would be uninsured for months."], ["They are automatically insured comprehensively, but still without any WAM cover at all.", "WAM cover is the minimum that applies to every vehicle on the policy."]], "1h.2"],
["Which combination of covers is characteristic of a cyber insurance?", [["First-party loss (such as data recovery and standstill) plus liability in the event of a data breach.", "A cyber policy combines first-party loss with liability and often also offers immediate assistance in the event of an incident."], ["Only damage to computers and servers caused by fire, water or burglary.", "Physical damage to equipment falls under contents or electronics insurance."], ["Only liability towards customers, without cover for own loss.", "A large part of cyber loss is in fact own loss, such as recovery and standstill."], ["Only fines imposed by the Dutch Data Protection Authority (Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens) after a data breach.", "Fines are insured at most to a limited extent and only where legally permitted; they are not the core of the cover."]], "1c.2"],
["An advisory firm discovers that personal data of clients has been taken in a hack. Within what period must the data breach as a rule be reported to the Dutch Data Protection Authority (Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens)?", [["Without undue delay and, where possible, within 72 hours of discovery.", "This follows from the GDPR (AVG), unless the breach is unlikely to result in a risk to the persons concerned; cyber insurers often help with this notification."], ["Within 30 days of discovering the breach, via the AP's reporting desk.", "That period is too generous; the GDPR (AVG) requires notification where possible within 72 hours."], ["Only in the firm's annual privacy report.", "A data breach must be reported immediately, not only afterwards in an annual report."], ["Only after the cyber insurer has given express permission for this.", "The reporting obligation is a legal duty of the controller and does not depend on the insurer."]], "4a.2"],
["Why do cyber insurers at acceptance often ask about measures such as multi-factor authentication and offline back-ups?", [["Because they greatly reduce likelihood and impact, and so affect premium and acceptance.", "Cyber risk depends heavily on basic security; the absence of such measures can lead to a higher premium, additional conditions or refusal."], ["Because these measures are legally prescribed for every company that works with data.", "There is no general legal obligation for exactly these measures for all companies; it concerns the insurer's acceptance policy."], ["Only for statistical purposes; the answers have no influence on the policy conditions at all.", "The answers in fact have a direct influence on acceptance, premium and conditions."], ["In order to be able to exclude the cover for own loss in advance of any incident.", "The questions serve to assess the risk, not to exclude the cover."]], "1f.1"],
["Logistics service provider Van Rijn in Venlo has 150 employees and an extensive cyber insurance. On a Friday afternoon, an employee in the finance department receives an email that appears to come from the director, with an urgent request to transfer €95,000 for a confidential takeover. She makes the payment, but it turns out to be a scam. The company's systems were not hacked. Why is this loss not automatically covered under the cyber policy?", [["Such CEO fraud is often excluded or limited: there is no hack, but a payment made by the company.", "For CEO fraud and social engineering a separate module or fraud insurance is sometimes needed; the adviser must read the conditions carefully."], ["This loss falls in all cases under Van Rijn's business liability insurance (AVB) for damage.", "The AVB covers liability for loss suffered by third parties, not the company's own financial loss through fraud."], ["This loss is in fact covered on every cyber policy without any limitation or sub-limit at all.", "That is not so; many policies exclude this form of fraud or have a low sub-limit."], ["The bank is legally obliged always to reimburse such a fraudulent business payment in full.", "A business customer who authorises a payment itself cannot, as a rule, rely on such a reimbursement obligation of the bank."]], "1e.2"],
["When, under the European rules, does an insured with a legal expenses insurance in any case have the right to a free choice of lawyer?", [["As soon as proceedings are needed, and in the event of a conflict of interest.", "Under the European rules and the case law of the Court of Justice, the insurer may not restrict the choice of lawyer in those situations."], ["Never; the insurer always decides itself which lawyer or counsel handles the case in substance.", "Free choice of lawyer is in fact a right guaranteed at European level in proceedings and conflicts of interest."], ["Only if the insurer agrees to it in writing in advance of the proceedings concerned.", "The right to free choice does not depend on the insurer's consent."], ["From the first contact, so even before advice and amicable negotiations have begun.", "In the phase before proceedings, the insurer may in principle have the case handled by its own lawyers."]], "2e.10"],
["Printing firm Peters in Nijmegen has eighteen employees and has been in dispute for months with a supplier over a new printing press that keeps breaking down. The supplier refuses to take the press back and threatens to collect the last instalment. After an angry exchange of emails, owner Ruud Peters quickly takes out a commercial legal expenses insurance. A week later he reports the conflict. What is the likely consequence?", [["No cover, because the dispute already existed or was foreseeable when the policy was taken out.", "Insurance covers uncertain future events; existing or foreseeable disputes are excluded."], ["Full cover, because the dispute was only reported to the insurer after the policy start date.", "What matters is when the dispute arose or was foreseeable, not when it is reported."], ["Cover once Ruud has paid the excess / deductible or own contribution under the policy.", "An excess / deductible does not change the exclusion of a dispute that already existed."], ["Cover, provided the financial interest of the dispute stays below the policy limit.", "A minimum or maximum interest regulates the scope, not the exclusion of existing disputes."]], "1e.2"],
["Why is the claims handling of legal expenses insurance placed in a separate legal entity at many insurers?", [["To prevent conflicts of interest; this follows from European rules.", "For example where the same insurer also insures the opposing party; the European rules require a safeguard such as a separate claims handling organisation."], ["For tax reasons, because legal expenses insurance then does not fall under insurance premium tax.", "The separation has a supervisory reason: preventing conflicts of interest."], ["Because Kifid prescribes this as a condition for the insurer's affiliation.", "Kifid is a complaints institute and does not impose that requirement; the rule comes from European legislation."], ["To make the costs, and thus the premium for legal expenses, lower.", "Cost control is not the reason; it is about independent handling of the case."]], "1c.4"],
["What do many commercial legal expenses policies provide when the financial interest of a dispute is much smaller than the expected handling costs?", [["The insurer may buy off the case by compensating the financial interest.", "With such a buy-out arrangement the insurer avoids disproportionately high costs; the insured then receives the interest in place of the proceedings."], ["The insurer must in that case always conduct the case up to the highest instance.", "Policies in fact contain proportionality considerations; there is no unlimited obligation to litigate."], ["The insured must then insure the dispute itself through the AVB.", "The AVB covers liability for loss suffered by third parties and not the insured's own legal disputes."], ["The cover lapses and the premium is refunded.", "The cover does not lapse; the insurer chooses a different way of compensating the interest."]], "2e.10"],
["A manufacturing company has a turnover of €2,400,000, variable costs of €1,500,000, fixed costs of €700,000 and a net profit of €200,000. What is the insurable interest in gross profit?", [["€900,000, both as turnover minus variable costs and as fixed costs plus net profit.", "With the subtractive method (€2,400,000 − €1,500,000) and the additive method (€700,000 + €200,000) you arrive at the same amount."], ["€700,000, because only the fixed costs continue to run during a stoppage of the business.", "Besides the fixed costs, gross profit also includes the net profit that is lost during a stoppage."], ["€2,200,000, i.e. turnover reduced by the net profit of the company in question.", "That calculation has no meaning; gross profit is turnover minus variable costs."], ["€1,500,000, because the variable costs in fact continue during a stoppage of the company.", "Variable costs largely fall away during a stoppage and therefore do not form part of gross profit."]], "1e.19"],
["What does an absence insurance for employers cover?", [["The wage costs the employer must keep paying during sickness, after the excess / deductible.", "It is a non-life insurance for the employer: the insurer compensates (part of) the wage costs paid."], ["The employee's income, which the insurer pays directly to the employee during sickness.", "The employee keeps the right to wages from the employer; the insurance compensates the employer, not the employee."], ["The employee's WIA benefit after two years of illness, paid by the insurer to the employee.", "After two years it concerns the WIA; separate insurances exist for that (for employers bearing their own risk)."], ["The transition payment on dismissal of a long-term sick employee, paid by the employer.", "A dismissal payment is not an absence risk and does not fall under an absence insurance."]], "1c.12"],
["Which costs fall under a reconstruction cost insurance in most policies?", [["Recreating records, archives, drawings and data after a covered loss.", "A reconstruction cost insurance compensates the costs of recovering or rebuilding lost information."], ["The loss of gross profit for as long as the records are unavailable after a fire.", "That loss of turnover belongs under a business interruption insurance, not under reconstruction costs."], ["The costs of rebuilding the office building in which the archives were stored.", "Rebuilding the building falls under the buildings insurance."], ["Claims from clients whose files were lost in a fire.", "Liability towards clients falls under a liability or professional liability insurance."]], "1e.23"],
["Which obligation under the Working Conditions Act (Arbowet) is relevant when assessing employer's liability and the acceptance of an AVB?", [["An employer with staff must have a risk inventory and evaluation (RI&E).", "A missing or outdated RI&E makes it difficult for the employer to show that it has complied with its duty of care."], ["An employer must have its company cars inspected annually by the Labour Inspectorate.", "Vehicle inspections have their own regime and are not a core obligation under the Arbowet."], ["An employer is legally required to take out a business liability insurance.", "An AVB is not legally compulsory for most companies, although it is strongly advisable."], ["An employer with staff must always have taken out an absence insurance.", "An absence insurance is voluntary; the employer may also bear the risk of continued wage payment itself."]], "1c.6"],
["Earthmoving company Hulshof in Hardenberg has twenty employees and its own machinery. Director Wim Hulshof phones his adviser on Monday morning: over the weekend a new mini excavator was stolen from the building site. The machine was bought two weeks ago but has not yet been reported to the insurer. Wim asks the adviser to report the machine with a start date of last week. What must the adviser do?", [["Refuse and report the mini excavator only with the actual date; the theft is then not covered.", "Backdating a start date to get a known loss covered is fraud; the adviser must act with integrity and take this signal seriously."], ["Carry out the request, because the client had already bought the machine before the theft.", "The moment of purchase does not change the fact that the loss was already established at the time of reporting; backdating is misleading."], ["Carry out the request, but inform the insurer of the theft only after acceptance.", "The adviser would thereby actively assist in misleading the insurer."], ["Report the mini excavator as of last week, provided Wim accepts a higher excess / deductible.", "A higher excess / deductible does not make backdating an insurance after a known loss any less misleading."]], "4a.1"],
["A holder of a binding authority (gevolmachtigde) handles a claim on behalf of the insurer and wrongly rejects it. Who is obliged to perform the insurance contract towards the policyholder?", [["The insurer, because the authority holder acted in its name.", "The contract was concluded with the insurer; it remains responsible, also for activities it has outsourced."], ["Only the authority holder, because it took the decision.", "The authority holder is a representative and not a contracting party as the insurer is."], ["The policyholder's adviser.", "The adviser has its own duty of care, but is not a party to the insurance contract."], ["Kifid, if the complaint is lodged there.", "Kifid resolves disputes, but is not a party that has to perform the contract."]], "1i.6"],
["What is a group contract in non-life and absence insurance?", [["A framework agreement under which members or employees of a group are insured.", "Group contracts offer scale: often more favourable conditions or premiums for the affiliated group."], ["A policy in which several insurers each bear a fixed share of one risk.", "That is co-insurance; a group contract is about insuring a group."], ["An agreement under which an insurer reinsures part of its own risks.", "Reinsurance takes place between insurers and is separate from insuring a group collectively."], ["A contract that can only be used for collective pension schemes.", "Group contracts also exist for, among other things, absence, accidents and liability."]], "1c.12"],
["Gardener Dirk Schouten has a business with five employees and is a member of a trade association for landscape gardeners. Through a group contract it offers a package policy with a substantial group discount. Dirk also prunes trees along public roads and regularly works with an aerial work platform. His adviser is considering placing him in the group contract. What is correct?", [["The adviser must still check whether the conditions suit Dirk's risks and wishes.", "A discount says nothing about the suitability of the cover; the duty of care and the assessment of wishes and needs continue to apply, especially for unusual activities."], ["With a group contract the duty of care lapses, because the trade association has already assessed the conditions.", "The trade association does not advise the individual client; that responsibility lies with the adviser."], ["A trade association's group contract is always better than an individual policy.", "Group conditions are standard and do not always match the client's specific risk."], ["The adviser only needs to compare the premium with that of other providers.", "Suitable advice is about cover, exclusions and conditions, not just price."]], "1c.12"],
["What is co-insurance?", [["Several insurers each directly bear a fixed share of the same risk.", "In co-insurance each participating insurer is the risk carrier for its own share towards the policyholder."], ["An insurer transfers part of the risk to one or more reinsurers.", "In reinsurance the policyholder has a contract only with the direct insurer."], ["Two separately concluded policies with different insurers for the same interest.", "That is double insurance, which is usually not coordinated; co-insurance is a deliberately divided placement."], ["The policyholder bears part of each loss itself as an excess / deductible.", "An excess / deductible is retention of risk by the policyholder, not a division between insurers."]], "1f.1"],
["With a co-insurance policy, one of the participating insurers goes bankrupt. What does this mean for the policyholder?", [["The others pay only their own share; not the share of the bankrupt insurer.", "Co-insurers are not jointly and severally liable; each is bound only for the share it has signed."], ["The other insurers automatically take over the bankrupt insurer's share.", "There is no joint and several liability; without a special agreement the others do not take over that share."], ["The leading insurer pays the full loss amount to the policyholder.", "The leader manages the policy but bears only its own share of the risk."], ["The policy lapses in its entirety retroactively to the start date.", "The policy continues to exist for the shares of the other insurers."]], "1i.6"],
["What role does the leading insurer have in a market (subscription) policy?", [["It carries out administration and claims handling on behalf of all participants.", "Through the leading-insurer clause its decisions bind the following insurers, to the extent the policy so provides; the policyholder does not have to negotiate with each insurer separately."], ["It always bears 100% of the risk and reinsures that with the following insurers.", "The leader bears only its own share; the following insurers are independent risk carriers for their share."], ["It only collects the premium; each insurer decides separately on claims.", "The leader in fact has a central role in claims handling, within the limits of the policy."], ["It is the broker that places the risk on the market on behalf of the client.", "The broker places the risk; the leading insurer is one of the risk carriers."]], "1f.1"],
["For a new commercial client, an adviser requests provisional cover pending the insurer's assessment. What is correct?", [["The client is covered immediately; the insurer can end the provisional cover if it does not accept.", "Provisional cover bridges the assessment period; if the risk is rejected, that cover ends, usually after a short period."], ["The client is only covered once the insurer has drawn up the policy definitively.", "The very purpose of provisional cover is that the client is covered during the assessment."], ["Because of the provisional cover the insurer is obliged to accept the risk definitively.", "The insurer retains the right to refuse after assessment or to impose other conditions."], ["Provisional cover applies only to loss that the client reports after definitive acceptance.", "Loss during the assessment period also falls under the provisional cover."]], "1f.1"],
["Why are large commercial risks often placed on the market through a broker?", [["Because the risk can thus be spread over several insurers, with tailor-made terms.", "A single insurer often has insufficient capacity or risk appetite; spreading through co-insurance solves that."], ["Because the conduct rules of the Financial Supervision Act (Wft) never apply on the market.", "That is too absolute; rules on intermediation and advice also apply to placement through the market."], ["Because it is legally prohibited for a single insurer to bear a large risk alone.", "No such prohibition exists; it is a matter of capacity and risk appetite."], ["Because underinsurance can by law never be applied under a market policy.", "Underinsurance depends on the policy conditions, not on whether it is a market policy."]], "1f.1"],
["What does the policyholder's duty of disclosure before the insurance is taken out entail?", [["The policyholder must disclose the facts it knows or ought to know and that may be relevant to the insurer.", "This is the core of the pre-contractual duty of disclosure in the BW; facts that are not expressly asked about may also fall under it."], ["The policyholder only has to disclose facts that are expressly asked about, even if no questionnaire was used at all.", "Without a questionnaire the general duty of disclosure applies; the limitation to questions asked belongs with an insurer's questionnaire."], ["The policyholder must report every change in the risk to the insurer immediately throughout the policy term.", "The statutory duty of disclosure concerns the period before the contract is concluded; duties to report during the term follow from the policy."], ["The policyholder only has to disclose facts it knows for certain, not facts it ought to have known.", "Facts it ought to know also fall under the duty; ignorance through negligence does not protect."]], "1f.1"],
["An insurer used its own questionnaire for an AVB. It was not asked about previous bankruptcies of the director. Can the insurer later rely on their non-disclosure?", [["No, unless the fact was withheld with the intent to mislead the insurer.", "With an insurer's questionnaire, the risk of questions not asked is for the insurer's account, except in the case of intent to mislead."], ["Yes, because the policyholder must always disclose all relevant facts of its own accord.", "When a questionnaire is used, the duty of disclosure is in principle limited to the questions asked."], ["Yes, provided the fact was relevant to acceptance.", "Relevance is not enough; without a question the insurer can only rely on it in the case of intent to mislead."], ["No, not even if it was withheld with intent to mislead.", "Intent to mislead is in fact the exception; the insurer can then rely on the non-disclosure."]], "1g.3"],
["After a loss it turns out that the policyholder failed to disclose facts when the insurance was taken out. The insurer would have concluded the insurance, but at a higher premium. What is the consequence?", [["The payment is reduced in proportion to the premium paid compared with the premium due.", "The BW opts for proportionality: the insured receives what corresponds to the premium actually paid."], ["Nothing is paid, because the policyholder breached its duty of disclosure.", "A full rejection fits the situation where the insurer would not have concluded the insurance at all, or intent to mislead."], ["The loss is paid in full, because the insurer would have concluded the insurance anyway.", "The insurer would have asked a higher premium; that difference is reflected in a lower payment."], ["The loss is paid in full, minus the premium difference over the policy term.", "The law applies a proportional reduction of the payment, not a settlement of the premium difference."]], "1i.10"],
["Electronics shop Yilmaz in Rotterdam has three employees. When the contents insurance was taken out, the insurer asked about previous losses, but owner Murat Yilmaz did not mention a burglary two years earlier. A year later lightning strikes the building and part of the stock is damaged beyond repair. The insurer then discovers the earlier burglary. May the insurer refuse the payment for the lightning strike on the ground of the non-disclosure?", [["No; the undisclosed fact is unrelated to the loss, so the insurer must in principle pay.", "The BW has a causation rule: an undisclosed fact that is not relevant to the risk as it materialised does not lead to a reduction, except in the case of intent to mislead."], ["Yes; any non-disclosure, even of a fact unrelated to the loss, justifies a refusal.", "The law sets limits on the consequences of non-disclosure, including the requirement of a connection with the loss that materialised."], ["Yes, but the insurer may then compensate only half of the damage to the stock.", "The law has no fixed halving; the consequences depend on causation and on what the insurer would have done."], ["No, because non-disclosure can never have consequences in commercial insurance.", "The rules on the duty of disclosure also apply to commercial insurance."]], "1g.3"],
["Within what period after discovering a breach of the duty of disclosure must the insurer point this out to the policyholder in order to be able to rely on the consequences?", [["Within two months.", "The insurer must point out the non-compliance within two months of discovery, stating the possible consequences; if it does not, it can no longer rely on it."], ["Within fourteen days.", "That period is shorter than the law provides."], ["Within six months.", "That period is longer than the law provides."], ["Within one year.", "The statutory period is considerably shorter, so that the policyholder quickly gets clarity."]], "1g.3"],
["Furniture maker Hout & Co in Zeist has ten employees and a workshop full of machines and tools. The contents have been insured for years for €150,000, but after investments the actual value has risen to €200,000. No guarantee against underinsurance and no first-loss cover has been agreed. In a fire in part of the workshop, €40,000 of damage is caused to the contents. How much does the insurer pay when applying the proportionality rule, without an excess / deductible?", [["€30,000, i.e. 75% of the loss.", "The cover is 75% (€150,000 / €200,000), so 75% of €40,000 is compensated."], ["€40,000, i.e. the full loss.", "That would apply with a first-loss insurance or with a guarantee against underinsurance, and those have not been agreed here."], ["€0, because there is underinsurance.", "Underinsurance leads to a proportional reduction, not to a full rejection."], ["€22,500, i.e. 75% of 75% of the loss.", "Here the 75% ratio has been applied twice; the rule is applied only once."]], "1i.7"],
["What does it mean if contents are insured 'on first-loss basis'?", [["The loss is compensated up to the sum insured, without reliance on underinsurance.", "With a first-loss insurance the actual value does not matter as long as the loss stays within the sum insured."], ["Only the first loss in an insurance year is covered; later losses are not.", "First loss says nothing about the number of losses per year."], ["The insurer pays only the part of the loss above the excess / deductible.", "That applies to every policy with an excess / deductible; the concept of first loss is about underinsurance."], ["In the case of double insurance this insurer always pays out first.", "The order in the event of concurrence is regulated through concurrence provisions, not through the concept of first loss."]], "1e.7"],
["A company has insured its contents with insurer A on a first-loss basis up to €100,000 and with insurer B on a second-loss basis. When does insurer B come into play?", [["For the part of the loss above the first-loss amount.", "The second-loss insurance is a supplementary layer above the first loss, under that policy's own conditions."], ["Always first, after which insurer A pays any remainder.", "The order is in fact the reverse: first loss first, then second loss."], ["For half of every loss, alongside insurer A.", "There is no split in half; each insurer covers a layer of its own of the loss."], ["Only if insurer A is bankrupt or does not pay for some other reason.", "Second loss is a layer of cover and not a safety net for the first insurer's insolvency."]], "1e.7"],
["An item is insured with two insurers for the same interest against the same risk, without special concurrence clauses. What is the main rule?", [["Each insurer is bound as if it were the only one, but in total not beyond the loss.", "The insured may choose whom to claim from; the insurers divide the burden among themselves in proportion, so that the principle of indemnity is preserved."], ["The insured can claim the full loss from both insurers and thereby receives it twice over.", "A non-life insurance may not lead to enrichment; the total payment is limited to the loss."], ["Only the insurer with the oldest policy is bound to compensate the loss of the insured.", "The law does not place the burden on the oldest policy; each insurer is in principle bound."], ["Both insurers automatically pay half and the insured may not choose which one to claim from.", "The insured may in fact choose whom to claim from; the division between the insurers follows afterwards."]], "1e.4"],
["At carpentry factory Hendriks in Doetinchem, with 35 employees, a fire starts in the evening in the spray shop. The manager extinguishes it with ten powder extinguishers and has a haulage company immediately fetch the ready frames from the adjacent hall. As a result the damage stays limited to the spray shop. The extinguishing costs and the transport together cost €6,500, while the sum insured for the contents has almost been reached through the fire damage. How are these costs compensated under the BW?", [["As reasonable costs of salvage, if necessary also above the sum insured.", "Costs of reasonable measures to prevent or limit imminent loss are for the insurer's account under the BW, even if the sum insured is thereby exceeded."], ["Not, because the factory took the measures itself without consulting the insurer.", "With an acute threat there is no need to consult first; prompt salvage is in fact desirable."], ["Only to the extent that room remains within the sum insured after the fire damage.", "Under the BW, salvage costs are also compensated above the sum insured."], ["Only the extinguishing agents, not the costs of the haulage company engaged.", "Moving items to safety to prevent loss is also salvage."]], "1i.13"],
["The insurer pays out a fire loss caused by a neighbour's contractor. What happens to the insured's claim against that contractor?", [["It passes to the insurer to the extent it has paid out (subrogation).", "The insurer can then seek recourse itself against the liable third party; the insured cannot be compensated twice."], ["It remains entirely with the insured, who can therefore still claim from the contractor.", "The insured would then be compensated twice; for the part paid out the claim passes to the insurer."], ["It lapses because of the payment.", "The claim does not lapse, but passes to the insurer."], ["It passes to the Guarantee Fund.", "The Motor Traffic Guarantee Fund (Waarborgfonds Motorverkeer) is only involved in traffic damage without insurance or with an unknown cause."]], "1i.6"],
["A hairdresser with a sole proprietorship has little insurance knowledge and asks for advice on business insurance. What does this mean for the adviser's duty of care?", [["The less expert the client, the more actively the adviser must warn.", "The duty of care depends on the circumstances; for a non-professional business client it extends further than for a professional buyer."], ["A business client is always regarded as professional, so the duty of care does not apply.", "A duty of care also applies to business clients; a small business owner is far from always an expert."], ["The adviser only needs to answer questions that the client asks itself.", "The adviser must also, of its own accord, flag relevant risks that the client does not know."], ["Sending the policy conditions is sufficient to meet the duty of care.", "Sending conditions does not replace explanation; the adviser must actively explain important limitations."]], "3c.3"],
["Metalworker Van Leeuwen in Eindhoven has had a continuing advisory relationship with its insurance agency for ten years. When the policies were taken out, the company had twelve employees and a modest set of machinery. Meanwhile thirty people work there and more than €1 million has been invested in new welding robots and a second hall. The sums insured have not been adjusted since. What follows from the adviser's duty of care?", [["The adviser must check whether the sums insured still fit and point out the underinsurance risk.", "A continuing advisory relationship includes periodic review; especially after large investments the adviser must actively flag this."], ["Nothing, because the client is itself responsible for reporting all of its new investments to the insurer.", "The client must report changes, but in a continuing relationship the adviser also has a duty to flag."], ["The adviser only needs to act once a loss has occurred and underinsurance is applied to the claim.", "Then it is too late; the duty of care is precisely about preventing underinsurance."], ["The adviser must cancel the policies immediately and have new quotations drawn up for the client.", "Cancelling is not an obvious step; first the sums insured and covers must be assessed."]], "2f.1"],
["Installation firm Van Vliet in Delft has twenty employees and so far only installed climate systems. Since this year the company has also made paid designs and energy advice for offices, without installing itself. The company's adviser hears this during an annual meeting. The company only has a business liability insurance (AVB). What must the adviser do?", [["Point out the lack of cover for advisory errors and discuss a professional liability insurance.", "A design error often leads to pure financial loss for the client, which is usually not covered under an AVB; a professional liability insurance can cover it."], ["Do nothing, because the AVB covers all liability arising from the conduct of the whole business.", "The AVB mainly covers personal injury and property damage; pure financial loss caused by advisory errors is usually outside the cover."], ["Advise replacing the AVB with a legal expenses insurance for disputes with clients of the firm.", "Legal expenses insurance helps with disputes, but pays no compensation to clients."], ["Only increase the AVB's sum insured, so that large advisory claims by clients also fit in it.", "A higher amount does not help if the loss falls outside the AVB's cover."]], "1c.17"],
["After advice, a client deliberately decides not to take out a business interruption insurance. Why is it important to record this in the file?", [["To be able to show that the client was warned of the risk and chose deliberately.", "A good file is, in the event of a complaint or liability claim, the evidence that the adviser met its duty of care."], ["Because the insurer must approve the adviser's advice file in advance of the advice.", "The advice file belongs to the adviser and does not have to be approved by an insurer."], ["Because the client thereby waives all possible future claims against the adviser concerned.", "Recording is evidence of the advice given, not a waiver of rights by the client."], ["Because the client can then no longer take out the insurance later on at all.", "The client can still insure later; recording does not restrict that possibility."]], "2f.8"],
["Under the IDD rules, what must a distributor in any case do before concluding any non-life insurance, also for a business client?", [["Establish the client's demands and needs and offer a product that matches them.", "This demands-and-needs test applies to all insurance distribution, with or without advice."], ["Make a premium comparison between at least three different insurers.", "The IDD does not require a comparison of three insurers; the core is matching demands and needs."], ["Have the client declare that it has read the policy conditions in full.", "Such a declaration does not replace the distributor's duty to establish demands and needs."], ["Nothing in particular, because the IDD applies only to life insurance.", "The IDD applies to all insurance distribution, so also to non-life insurance."]], "2e.4"],
["After a fire, a wholesaler temporarily rents another hall in order to keep supplying its customers. How are those extra costs compensated under a business interruption insurance in most policies?", [["As costs to limit the business interruption, up to the amount by which the loss is reduced.", "Extra costs that limit the loss of gross profit are compensated to the extent they are recouped by a lower business interruption loss."], ["Not, because a business interruption insurance compensates only the loss of gross profit.", "Costs that limit the business interruption are in fact compensated because they ultimately save the insurer money."], ["In full and without limit, even if they are much higher than the business interruption loss they prevent.", "Without additional-expenses cover, these costs are as a rule limited to the saving on the business interruption loss."], ["Only if the wholesaler took out a separate reconstruction cost insurance beforehand.", "Reconstruction costs concern the recovery of records and data, not temporary accommodation."]], "1i.26"],
["After a fire, the insurer discovers that a private limited company (bv) deliberately concealed an earlier fire when taking out the buildings insurance, in order to mislead the insurer. Can the insurer terminate the insurance?", [["Yes, within two months of discovery, and with immediate effect in the case of intent to mislead.", "In the case of breach of the duty of disclosure, the BW gives the insurer a right to terminate within two months of discovery; with intent to mislead this may be done immediately."], ["No, a commercial non-life insurance can only be terminated at the contract expiry date of it.", "In the case of breach of the duty of disclosure the law gives its own right of termination, independent of the contract expiry date."], ["Yes, but only if it refunds the premium for the entire term to the bv in question.", "In the case of intent to mislead, the insurer in principle does not have to refund the premium."], ["No, the insurer can only reduce the payment that it makes for this particular fire.", "Besides the consequences for the payment, the insurer also has the right to terminate the insurance."]], "1a.1"],
["A business owner disagrees with the loss assessment by the insurer's expert and engages its own counter-expert. Who in principle bears the costs of this under the BW?", [["The insurer, to the extent the costs are reasonable, even if the sum insured is exceeded.", "Reasonable costs of establishing the loss and the payment are for the insurer's account under the BW."], ["Always the business owner itself, because it engages the counter-expert on its own initiative.", "The costs of its own expert can also be reasonable costs of loss assessment that the insurer compensates."], ["The business owner and the insurer each half, regardless of the amount of the costs.", "The law has no fixed split in half; what matters is whether the costs are reasonable."], ["The insurer, but only if the counter-expert is ultimately proved right.", "Compensation does not depend on who is proved right, but on the reasonableness of the costs."]], "1i.1"],
["An insurance agency intermediates exclusively in non-life insurance. Does it, as an intermediary, fall under the Anti-Money Laundering and Counter-Terrorist Financing Act (Wwft)?", [["No; the Wwft designates intermediaries in life insurance, but not in non-life.", "Sanctions legislation does apply to everyone; the agency must therefore still be alert to sanctioned persons."], ["Yes, every financial service provider with a licence from the Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM) falls under the Wwft.", "The Wwft applies to designated institutions; an AFM licence for non-life intermediation does not automatically make an agency subject to the Wwft."], ["Yes, but only for business clients and not for private individuals.", "The distinction between business and private does not determine whether the Wwft applies; the type of service does."], ["Only if the agency employs more than ten staff.", "The size of the agency does not determine whether it falls under the Wwft."]], "4a.1"],
["What is, in essence, the purpose of the insurers' code of conduct on informed renewal and contract terms for commercial non-life and income insurance?", [["That commercial clients are informed in good time before a renewal, so they can choose consciously.", "The code is meant to prevent a business owner from being unknowingly bound to a new contract period; it receives information on renewal and conditions beforehand."], ["That insurers may never again tacitly renew commercial contracts, whatever the circumstances.", "Tacit renewal remains possible; the code is about timely and clear information in advance."], ["That premium increases on commercial insurances at renewal are no longer permitted at all.", "The code does not prohibit premium adjustments; it is about informing the client."], ["That commercial clients get the same statutory termination rights as consumers do.", "A code of conduct does not change the law; the statutory right of termination after tacit renewal remains for consumers."]], "1a.3"],
["Painting company De Kwast vof in Gorinchem has two partners, Ahmed and Ruben, and six employees. During a job in an office building, work with a hot-air gun causes a large fire. The damage at the client is estimated at €3 million, while the AVB has a sum insured of €2.5 million per claim. The partners ask their adviser what the shortfall means for them. What is correct?", [["The partners are jointly and severally liable, also with their private assets.", "In a general partnership (vof) the partners are each liable for the whole of the firm's debts; private assets are not ring-fenced."], ["Only the vof's assets can be claimed against, in the same way as with a bv.", "A vof is not a legal entity with separate liability; the partners are themselves answerable for the debts."], ["Each partner is liable for at most half of the shortfall.", "Partners of a vof are jointly and severally liable; the creditor can claim the whole amount from either of them."], ["The insurer must pay the shortfall anyway, because the partners are privately liable for it.", "The insurer pays at most the sum insured; the shortfall is for the insured's account."]], "1b.1"],
["Bakery Van Doorn in Ede takes over the bakery of a retired colleague in Veenendaal. The purchase is an asset transaction: Van Doorn buys the premises, the ovens, the customer base and the brand name, and continues the business. Twelve employees work at the colleague's. Owner Bas van Doorn wants to make new, lower salary arrangements with a few of them. What applies in principle to these employees?", [["They transfer by operation of law to Van Doorn, with retention of their terms of employment.", "On a transfer of an undertaking, the rights and obligations under the employment contracts pass automatically to the acquirer."], ["They remain employed by the seller until they sign a new contract themselves.", "The employees transfer by operation of law; a new contract with the acquirer is not needed for that."], ["They transfer, but Van Doorn may freely adjust their salaries downwards on the takeover.", "The terms of employment in fact transfer unchanged; the takeover is no reason to lower them unilaterally."], ["Their employment ends by operation of law on the transfer of the assets.", "A transfer of an undertaking does not end the employment contracts; they continue with the acquirer."]], "1b.2"],
["While handling a damage claim it turns out that the UBO (ultimate beneficial owner) of the business client is on an EU sanctions list. What is correct?", [["Do not pay out, freeze the funds and report the hit in accordance with the sanctions rules.", "Paying to or for the benefit of a sanctioned person is prohibited, even if the claim is itself covered."], ["Make the payment as normal, because the claim falls within the policy cover.", "A covered claim does not make a payment permitted if sanctions rules prohibit it."], ["Make the payment as normal, provided the UBO concerned gives prior written consent.", "The consent of the sanctioned person does not change the prohibition."], ["Only withhold the excess / deductible and pay out the rest of the loss.", "A partial payment is equally contrary to the sanctions prohibition."]], "1i.2"],
["What does a sanctions clause in a commercial policy provide?", [["The insurer need not cover or pay if that would breach sanctions rules.", "The clause prevents the insurer from being contractually obliged to do something that is legally prohibited."], ["The policyholder pays a fine for late payment of the premium due under the policy.", "That concerns premium payment and has nothing to do with international sanctions."], ["The insurer may itself impose sanctions on the policyholder if it discovers fraud.", "Anti-fraud measures are something different; the sanctions clause concerns international sanctions legislation."], ["Loss in countries outside the EU is in all cases excluded from cover under the policy.", "The clause does not exclude countries as such, but only cover that conflicts with sanctions rules."]], "1i.2"],
["In non-life insurance, what is the difference between damage caused by precipitation and damage caused by flooding?", [["Precipitation comes from the air; flooding arises from failing or overflowing water defences.", "Precipitation is rain, snow, hail or meltwater that enters the building; flooding comes from the failure or overflowing of dykes, embankments or other water defences. Precipitation is usually covered, flooding is often excluded or insurable only to a limited extent."], ["Precipitation arises from a dyke breach or quay collapse; flooding is water from heavy rainfall.", "This is exactly the reverse."], ["There is no difference; both terms are used in policies without distinction.", "Policies in fact make a sharp distinction, with major consequences for cover."], ["Precipitation is only hail and snow; all other water is called flooding.", "Rain and meltwater also fall under precipitation."]], "1c.8"],
["When can a business owner receive compensation under the Compensation for Damage in Disasters Act (WTS)?", [["When the damage results from a designated disaster and was not reasonably insurable.", "The WTS is a safety net; damage that was reasonably insurable does not qualify for compensation."], ["Always, as a supplement to the payment the insurer has already made to the business owner.", "The WTS does not supplement an insurance payment; it is intended for uninsurable damage."], ["Even if the damage was insurable, but the business owner had not taken out any insurance.", "Whoever does not insure an insurable risk cannot rely on the WTS."], ["Only if it concerns damage to a private home, and not to the premises of a business.", "Business owners can also qualify under conditions."]], "1e.2"],
["Wholesaler Davelaar Tuinmeubelen in Barneveld has a company hall of 4,000 square metres. Owner Henk Davelaar has the entire roof covered with solar panels and places a large battery for energy storage in the hall. The investment amounts to more than €300,000. He sees this mainly as a sustainability project and does not think about his insurances. What is relevant for his adviser?", [["The risk changes; report this and reassess the sum insured and prevention requirements.", "Solar panels and battery storage increase the value and may change the fire risk; insurers often impose requirements on installation and inspection."], ["Nothing, because solar panels on the roof, as part of the building, are always automatically co-insured.", "Even if they belong to the building, the sum insured may become too low and the insurer may impose requirements."], ["Only the yield of the electricity generated has to be reported to the business interruption insurance.", "The most important thing is the change in the material risk and the value; that goes beyond the yield."], ["The battery does not have to be reported, because energy storage has no influence on the fire risk.", "Battery storage can in fact bring its own fire risk, on which insurers often impose requirements."]], "2f.1"],
["Investment company Hofstede Vastgoed in Utrecht owns a four-storey office building in Nieuwegein. The last tenant has left and the building has been empty for eight months, with no new tenant in sight. Director Ellen Hofstede has let the buildings insurance continue unchanged and has not reported anything to the insurer. Her adviser hears about it by chance during a conversation. What should he above all put to her?", [["Report the vacancy; in most policies, restrictions or additional conditions apply in the case of long-term vacancy.", "Vacancy increases the likelihood of, among other things, squatting, vandalism and arson; many policies have a reporting duty and then restrict the cover."], ["Nothing, because a vacant building actually represents a smaller risk for an insurer.", "An empty building has no daily supervision, so the risk of squatting, vandalism and arson in fact increases."], ["Cancel the buildings insurance immediately, because without a tenant there is no longer an insurable interest.", "The owner retains an insurable interest in the building; vacancy calls for adjustment, not for cancellation."], ["Only have the departed tenant's contents insurance transferred into Hofstede's name.", "The tenant's contents are not Hofstede's; the issue here is the risk for the vacant building itself."]], "1c.10"],
["Lieke de Graaf takes her passenger car to Autobedrijf Smeets in Roermond, which has eight mechanics, for a major service. A mechanic drives the car in the workshop into a vehicle lift, severely damaging the front. Lieke has a comprehensive insurance with a no-claim discount. The garage business has a garage insurance that covers damage to customers' cars. Where is this loss as a rule best claimed?", [["From the garage business's garage insurer, so that Lieke's no-claim discount is left untouched.", "The garage business is liable for damage to the car it has in its custody; through the garage policy that loss is compensated without consequences for Lieke's own policy."], ["From Lieke's own-damage insurer, because that always takes precedence over the garage insurance.", "There is no rule that one's own comprehensive cover always takes precedence; claiming through the garage avoids loss of the no-claim discount."], ["From the garage business's WAM insurer, because the damage was caused with a motor vehicle.", "This concerns damage to the customer's own car, in the workshop; the garage insurance is intended for that."], ["Nowhere, because damage during maintenance is excluded on both comprehensive and garage insurances.", "Damage to customers' cars in the workshop is in fact a core risk of the garage insurance."]], "1i.22"],
["A business owner decides after a major fire not to continue the business. What does a business interruption insurance in most policies still compensate then?", [["Only the continuing fixed costs, as long as they cannot be terminated and within the indemnity period.", "On termination the net profit is lost; the policy then generally compensates the fixed charges that still continue."], ["The full gross profit over the entire indemnity period, as if the business were continued.", "Without continuation there is no recovery period in which gross profit is missed; the compensation is therefore more limited."], ["The expected net profit over the years until the business owner's planned retirement date.", "A business interruption insurance never compensates profit over a period beyond the indemnity period."], ["Nothing, because termination of the business causes any claim for business interruption to lapse.", "Even on termination, continuing fixed costs can be compensated in most policies."]], "1i.25"],
["At printing firm Van Beek in Zutphen, with thirty employees, a fire breaks out on Sunday night in the paper store. The fire brigade extinguishes the fire, but the roof has partly collapsed and the extinguishing water stands among the printing presses. Still during the night, a salvage coordinator arrives on site at the fire brigade's request. Owner Harm van Beek asks what he is there to do and who pays for it. What is correct?", [["He takes measures on behalf of the insurers at once to limit further loss; the costs are as a rule theirs.", "Salvage provides first aid after the fire, such as covering, securing and protecting machines; the insurers as a rule bear these costs."], ["He sells the damaged stock immediately to secure the residual value for the insurer concerned.", "Salvage here is about limiting further loss after the fire, not about selling remnants."], ["He establishes whether the fire was caused by the owner's intent or negligence in the company.", "Investigation of the cause belongs to the loss adjuster's work and possibly the police, not to the salvage coordinator."], ["He works on Harm's instructions, who must advance the costs himself and claim them later from the insurer.", "The salvage service works for the insurers; the insured does not as a rule have to advance the costs."]], "1i.19"],
["Web agency Jansen Digitaal bv in Arnhem has six employees, but runs almost entirely on director-major shareholder Lotte Jansen. She maintains all client contacts, sells the projects and is the only one who knows the most important systems. The bv has an AVB, a contents policy and a cyber insurance. During the annual risk inventory the adviser asks where the greatest vulnerability lies. Which risk deserves particular attention here?", [["Loss of Lotte as a key person through illness, incapacity for work or death.", "If the only key person is lost, turnover can disappear; think of key person, incapacity for work or death cover, and of succession."], ["Damage to the contents through fire, because that is the largest financial risk for a web agency.", "For a web agency the contents are usually limited; the greatest risk here lies in the person."], ["Liability for injury to visitors in the bv's office.", "That risk exists, but for a web agency it is generally limited and already covered by the AVB."], ["Theft of laptops, because employees regularly take them home.", "A real but small risk, which does not threaten continuity in the way the loss of the key person does."]], "1c.1"],
["Why is fire compartmentation in a commercial building important to the insurer?", [["It limits the maximum loss per fire, which influences acceptance, capacity and premium.", "Fire-resistant partitions keep a fire within one compartment; the insurer looks at that maximum loss when determining its share and price."], ["It is a statutory condition before a fire insurance may be concluded.", "Building regulations set fire safety requirements, but concluding an insurance is not legally dependent on them."], ["It means the proportionality rule can no longer be applied.", "Underinsurance depends on sum insured and value, not on the building's structural layout."], ["It makes a separate business interruption insurance unnecessary for the company in all cases.", "A fire in one compartment can also bring the business to a halt; the need for business interruption cover remains."]], "1c.5"],
["What should the level of the excess / deductible on a client's commercial policies above all be aligned with?", [["The company's financial capacity and liquidity.", "An excess / deductible saves premium, but the business must actually be able to pay the amount for one or more losses."], ["The excess / deductible that the client's competitors apply.", "What competitors choose says nothing about what this company can absorb financially."], ["The lowest excess / deductible the insurer offers, regardless of the premium.", "The lowest possible excess / deductible is not always efficient; the client then pays small losses through a higher premium."], ["The amount of the largest loss the company has ever had.", "An earlier loss is an indication, but the question is what the company can bear financially now."]], "2e.6"],
["An adviser takes over a new commercial client who has had policies with various insurers for years. What is a sensible first step?", [["Make a policy analysis to find double cover and gaps relative to the risk profile.", "Policies taken out separately often overlap or leave risks uncovered; that only becomes apparent when you set them against the risk profile."], ["Cancel all existing policies immediately and take them out again with a single insurer.", "Cancelling without analysis can create gaps in cover, for example with claims-made policies or pending losses."], ["Let the policies continue unchanged, because the previous adviser remains responsible for his advice.", "Whoever takes over the client has its own duty of care for the advice from that moment."], ["Only compare the premiums and choose the cheapest provider.", "Premium says nothing about the suitability of the cover; first it must be clear what has to be covered."]], "2c.1"],
["A chemical company decides to stop a dangerous production line because the risk is not insurable and cannot be well controlled. Which form of risk treatment is this?", [["Risk avoidance.", "By ceasing the activity the risk disappears; that is avoidance, the most far-reaching form of risk treatment."], ["Risk transfer.", "With transfer the risk goes to someone else, for example an insurer or contract partner; here the risk in fact disappears."], ["Risk reduction.", "With reduction the activity continues and likelihood or size is reduced; here the activity stops."], ["Risk acceptance.", "Accepting means deliberately bearing the risk oneself; the company here chooses no longer to run the risk."]], "2d.2"],
["How is explosion described in essence in the customary standard definition in non-life policies?", [["A sudden, violent release of force through the expansion of gases or vapours.", "The core is the sudden and violent effect of gas or vapour pressure; a vessel that tears slowly does not fall under it."], ["Any crack in a tank or pipe through which a liquid escapes from the installation.", "Leakage or cracks without a sudden, violent release of force from gases or vapours is not an explosion."], ["Any loud bang, even if no damage is caused by a pressure wave following the bang.", "What matters is the release of force itself and the damage it causes, not the noise."], ["Exclusively an explosion caused by explosives or fireworks, not by gas in an installation.", "The definition is wider: a gas explosion in an installation also falls under it."]], "1c.8"],
["Administration firm Visser in Assen has eight employees and a contents insurance based on the customary fire conditions. During a severe thunderstorm lightning strikes a high-voltage pylon a few hundred metres away. Because of a surge on the grid, five computers and the server in the office are damaged. Lightning did not strike the premises themselves. What is correct?", [["This is not a lightning strike on the premises; surge damage is often only limitedly or separately covered.", "On many policies, surge damage from an indirect lightning strike falls under a separate, sometimes limited cover or under an electronics insurance."], ["This is a lightning strike within the meaning of the policy, because the damage arose during a thunderstorm.", "A lightning strike generally requires a strike on the insured object or its immediate surroundings; surge is something different."], ["This damage is never covered, because the cause lies outside the premises of the insured firm.", "That is too absolute; many policies cover surge, whether or not to a limited extent, and an electronics insurance can also cover it."], ["The grid operator is always fully liable for surge damage after a thunderstorm, whatever the cause.", "In a thunderstorm the grid operator is generally not liable; it concerns a natural phenomenon."]], "1e.1"],
["Because of a short circuit, the electric motor of a machine burns out, without any flames arising that spread further. Which insurance is most obvious for this loss?", [["A machinery breakdown insurance, because this is internal damage without fire.", "Without a fire that spreads independently there is no fire; the machinery breakdown policy in fact covers sudden damage from within."], ["The fire insurance, because a short circuit always counts as fire.", "A short circuit is a cause, not an event that automatically falls under the concept of fire; there are no spreading flames here."], ["The business liability insurance.", "The AVB covers damage to third parties; damage to one's own machine falls outside it."], ["No insurance at all, because a short circuit always counts as wear and tear.", "A short circuit is not by definition wear and tear; a sudden unforeseen failure can be covered under a machinery breakdown policy."]], "1c.9"],
["Which cause of loss typically falls under a machinery breakdown insurance?", [["An operating error or a sudden material or construction defect from within.", "Machinery breakdown covers sudden and unforeseen damage to the machine itself, also through own errors or internal causes."], ["Gradual wear from years of normal use.", "Wear is foreseeable and not an uncertain event; it falls under maintenance costs, not under the insurance."], ["Personal injury that a runaway machine causes to a visitor to the business.", "Injury to third parties falls under the liability insurance, not under a property insurance for the machine."], ["The loss of turnover because the machine stands still for weeks.", "That is business interruption; a separate (machinery) business interruption cover is needed for it."]], "1e.1"],
["What does an index clause on a buildings insurance for a commercial building entail?", [["The sum insured is adjusted periodically to an index of building costs.", "The sum insured thus grows with the rebuilding costs and the risk of underinsurance is limited."], ["The premium is reduced annually as long as no loss is reported.", "That is a form of no-claim discount; an index clause concerns the sum insured."], ["The payment is reduced after a loss by the inflation since the start date.", "The clause in fact works in the insured's favour by letting the sum grow along."], ["The insurer may adjust the conditions unilaterally to the market every year.", "That is an amendment clause for conditions; indexation concerns amounts."]], "1e.3"],
["An insurer offers a guarantee against underinsurance on a buildings policy. What does that mean?", [["The insurer, under conditions, waives the proportionality rule, often after a valuation.", "The policyholder gets certainty that a sum insured that is too low does not lead to a proportionally lower payment, as long as the agreements are met."], ["The insurer compensates any loss, even above the rebuilding value.", "The guarantee removes the proportionality rule, but the principle of indemnity continues to apply: no more than the actual loss."], ["The policyholder never has to report changes to the building again.", "Renovations or extensions often have to be reported; otherwise the guarantee may lapse."], ["The insurer guarantees that the premium will not rise during the entire policy term.", "It concerns the calculation of the payment, not a fixed premium."]], "1i.18"],
["Hotel De Zeeuwse Stroom in Vlissingen has forty rooms and 35 employees. Because the listed building is hard to value, owner Marieke Verburg had it valued by an expert. The insurer accepted the valuation report and aligned the rebuilding value on the buildings policy with it. Two years later a wing burns down. The insurer now states that the building was worth less than valued. What is correct?", [["The valued amount is in principle fixed, except, for example, in the case of fraud or incorrect information.", "An accepted expert valuation prevents discussion afterwards; as long as the valuation is valid, the insurer cannot simply contest that value again."], ["The insurer may have the value re-determined after each loss and then ignore the valuation.", "The valuation would then lose its function; it is in fact intended to prevent a discussion about value after a loss."], ["The insurer may reduce the payment at its own discretion, because valuations usually turn out too high.", "There is no general power to set aside an accepted valuation."], ["The valuation counts only if Marieke has rebuilt the wing within a year of the fire.", "The valuation concerns the value; any rebuilding arrangements are separate from it."]], "1i.15"],
["Timber merchant Brinkman in Meppel has its own shed and office and ten employees. After a major fire the shed is completely destroyed. Owner Jan Brinkman is 63 and decides not to rebuild the shed, but to sell the land and wind down his business. The buildings policy was taken out on a rebuilding value basis. On what basis is payment then made in most policies?", [["On a lower basis, such as the selling value, if that is lower than the rebuilding value.", "The rebuilding value compensates the costs of actually rebuilding; whoever does not rebuild does not incur those costs and often receives lower compensation."], ["Always on the rebuilding value, because that is stated as the sum insured on the buildings policy schedule.", "Many conditions link the rebuilding value to rebuilding or repair; without rebuilding a lower basis often applies."], ["On the WOZ value of the shed and land, because that value was set by the municipality.", "The WOZ value is a tax value including land and not a usual basis of payment under a buildings policy."], ["Nothing is paid, because rebuilding is always a condition for cover under the buildings policy.", "Not rebuilding generally leads to a different basis of calculation, not to the lapse of cover."]], "1i.17"],
["What is understood by clean-up costs on a commercial fire policy?", [["The costs of demolishing, clearing and removing remains after a covered loss.", "After a fire the debris must go before rebuilding can take place; these costs are often compensated on top of the sum insured up to a maximum."], ["The costs of keeping the building clean and well cleared before a loss as prevention.", "Preventive maintenance and keeping things tidy are costs of the business itself; clean-up costs follow a loss."], ["The costs of moving goods to safety during an incipient fire.", "Those are salvage costs: costs to prevent or limit imminent loss."], ["The costs of soil remediation, which are always compensated in full.", "Remediation of contaminated soil is often excluded or only limitedly covered."]], "1i.14"],
["Property company De Wit Beheer owns a commercial building in Ede that is let to a wholesaler of office supplies. The rent is €15,000 per month. After a major fire the building is unusable for eight months and, under the lease, the tenant no longer pays rent. The buildings policy compensates the rebuilding of the building. Which cover protects the landlord against the loss of rental income?", [["A loss of rent cover, often insured as an addition to the buildings policy.", "Loss of rent compensates the missed rent as long as the building is unusable due to a covered loss, usually up to a maximum period."], ["The tenant's business interruption insurance, because it continues paying the fixed costs.", "That insurance protects the tenant's interest, not the landlord's rental income."], ["The landlord's liability insurance for damage to third parties.", "An AVB covers liability towards third parties, not own loss of income."], ["No cover at all, because lost rent is an uninsurable business risk.", "Loss of rent is in fact readily insurable, as a supplement to the buildings insurance."]], "1c.3"],
["A business interruption policy has a waiting period of a number of days. What does that mean?", [["The loss over the first days after the loss is not compensated: an excess / deductible in time.", "The business owner bears short interruptions itself; the compensation starts after the waiting period has ended."], ["The insurer has that number of days to assess the loss and to send an expert to the company.", "A handling period is something different; the waiting period concerns the time span for which nothing is paid."], ["The insurance only starts that many days after it has been taken out by the policyholder.", "That is a waiting period at inception; the waiting period in business interruption counts from the moment of the loss."], ["The policyholder must report the loss to the insurer within that number of days.", "The reporting period is separate from the waiting period and does not determine the time span for which compensation is paid."]], "1e.2"],
["Plastics factory Lubbers in Emmen, with sixty employees, produces in a fully continuous process. Because of a fault at a substation of the grid operator, power is out across the whole region for two days. At the factory itself there is no material damage, but production is completely at a standstill. The company has a traditional fire business interruption insurance without extensions. Is the loss of gross profit covered under this policy?", [["As a rule not, unless failure of public utilities is co-insured.", "A traditional business interruption policy requires material damage at the insured by a covered event; utilities failure is a separate extension."], ["Yes, because any business standstill falls under a business interruption insurance.", "Standstill alone is not enough; there must be covered material damage."], ["Yes, but only if the fault lasted longer than the policy's waiting period.", "The waiting period limits the compensation, but does not make an uncovered cause covered."], ["No, because power failure is always excluded and cannot be co-insured either.", "Failure of utilities can in fact often be co-insured as an extension."]], "1e.2"],
["Car dealer Kremer in Hoorn has a showroom and a workshop with six mechanics. On average fifteen customers' cars are in the workshop for maintenance or repair. After a fire, seven customers' cars turn out to be a total loss. The garage's contents policy covers only its own contents and trade stock. What should the adviser have flagged?", [["That third-party property in its custody, such as customers' cars, must be insured separately.", "Customers' cars are not the garage's property; they are co-insured through a garage insurance or a cover for third-party property."], ["Nothing, because customers' own-damage insurances always compensate this loss in full.", "Not every customer has comprehensive cover, and the garage may itself be liable for property in its custody."], ["That the building's buildings policy automatically co-insures customers' cars.", "A buildings policy covers the building, not third parties' movable property inside it."], ["That the garage's AVB always covers this loss without a care, custody and control restriction.", "Third-party property in custody is in fact usually excluded or limitedly covered under an AVB."]], "1c.3"],
["From what wind speed is there a storm according to the customary standard definition in non-life policies?", [["At least 14 metres per second.", "That limit corresponds to wind force 7; below that speed wind damage does not count as storm damage."], ["At least 7 metres per second.", "Wind force 7 is something different from 7 metres per second; the definition places the limit at 14 metres per second."], ["At least 20 metres per second.", "That is around wind force 8 to 9; the storm definition begins lower."], ["Only when the Royal Netherlands Meteorological Institute (KNMI) has officially issued a code red.", "A weather warning is not part of the definition; it concerns the measured wind speed."]], "1c.8"],
["After a burglary at business premises the insurer states that it only pays if there are visible signs of forced entry. Why do burglary covers impose this requirement?", [["Signs of forced entry show that there really was a break-in, and not that items have gone missing.", "Without signs of forced entry it is hard to distinguish whether items were taken by burglary or disappeared in some other way."], ["Because without signs of forced entry the police do not take a report of burglary at business premises.", "A report can also be made without signs of forced entry; the requirement comes from the policy, not from the reporting rules."], ["Because loss through theft without forced entry always falls under the AVB.", "The AVB covers liability towards third parties, not the loss of own property."], ["Because the law provides that theft without forced entry may not be covered by an insurer.", "There is no statutory prohibition; it is a policy condition that insurers apply."]], "1i.2"],
["Why is an electronics insurance a sensible supplement to the contents insurance for an office with a lot of IT equipment?", [["It also covers sudden damage from within and through one's own actions, such as drop and impact damage.", "A contents policy mainly covers named events such as fire and burglary; an electronics policy covers more broadly, for example drop and impact damage."], ["Because a contents policy never covers electronic equipment.", "Equipment does fall under contents, but only for the named events; the difference lies in the breadth of the cover."], ["Because the electronics policy also covers liability towards clients for data breaches and hacks.", "Liability for a data breach belongs under a cyber or liability cover, not under a property insurance for equipment."], ["Because the law requires companies to insure their IT equipment separately.", "There is no statutory obligation; it is a choice based on the risk profile."]], "1c.9"],
["A defective coffee machine from a manufacturer causes a fire in a living room. Which property damage falls under the product liability regime of the BW?", [["Damage to other items for private use, such as the furnishings, above a threshold.", "The regime mainly protects consumers: damage to private property is covered, damage to the defective product itself is not."], ["Also the damage to the coffee machine itself, because that machine was defective and caused the fire.", "Damage to the product itself does not fall under product liability; for that the buyer can hold its seller liable."], ["Only damage to items used for business.", "The opposite is true: property damage is covered if the item is of a type ordinarily intended for private use and was so used."], ["Only personal injury; property damage never falls under the regime.", "Besides personal injury, the regime also covers damage to private property, albeit with a threshold."]], "1g.3"],
["Tool wholesaler Mensink in Groningen imports electric tools from a manufacturer in China and sells them to construction companies in the Netherlands. The company has fifteen employees. A circular saw turns out to have a construction defect, as a result of which a carpenter suffers serious injury. The Chinese manufacturer is practically impossible for the carpenter to claim against. What is correct?", [["The wholesaler, as importer into the EU, can be held liable as a producer.", "The BW treats whoever imports a product into the EU in order to sell it as a producer, so that the victim has a point of recourse within the EU."], ["Only the manufacturer outside the EU can be held liable under product liability.", "The regime in fact also designates the importer, in order to protect victims."], ["The wholesaler can only be liable if it knew of the defect.", "Product liability is strict liability; knowledge of the defect is not required."], ["The carpenter must turn to his own employer, who is always solely liable.", "The employer may be liable, but that does not exclude the importer's liability."]], "1g.3"],
["A producer defends itself with the argument that the defect could not be discovered given the state of scientific and technical knowledge at the time the product was put into circulation. What is this defence called?", [["The development risk defence.", "This is one of the producer's statutory defences: it is not liable if the defect could not be discovered with the knowledge of the time."], ["The contributory negligence defence.", "Contributory negligence concerns a fault of the injured party, which can reduce the compensation; that is something different."], ["The force majeure defence in breach of contract.", "Force majeure belongs to contractual liability; product liability has its own system of defences."], ["The limitation defence.", "Limitation concerns the passing of time after the loss, not whether the defect was discoverable."]], "1g.3"],
["Why are occupational diseases with a long latency period, such as asbestos-related diseases, difficult for cover under an AVB?", [["The disease often only manifests itself after many years, which raises questions about which policy covers it.", "With a long latency, the exposure, the onset of the disease and the claim may fall in different policy periods or with different insurers."], ["Occupational diseases by law never fall under employer's liability.", "An employer can be liable for occupational diseases if it has breached its duty of care."], ["Occupational diseases are always fully compensated by the WIA (Work and Income according to Labour Capacity Act), so no loss remains.", "The WIA compensates only part of the loss of income and no compensation for pain and suffering; loss remains."], ["Occupational diseases are by definition caused intentionally and therefore excluded.", "An employer rarely causes an occupational disease intentionally; the intent exclusion is usually not at issue here."]], "1e.2"],
["Contractor Bosman in Den Bosch, with forty employees, is renovating a shop building in the city centre. For the brickwork on the façade Bosman engages an independent bricklaying firm. An employee of that bricklaying firm drops a pallet of bricks from the scaffold onto a passer-by, who is seriously injured. The passer-by holds Bosman liable. Can Bosman be liable under the BW?", [["Yes, because the brickwork is carried out in the conduct of Bosman's business.", "The BW makes whoever has work performed by a non-subordinate in the conduct of its business liable towards third parties alongside that non-subordinate."], ["No, because only the bricklaying firm as the employee's employer can be held liable.", "The bricklaying firm is liable, but the BW also makes the principal liable in this situation."], ["No, because Bosman itself made no mistake at all in the work on the façade.", "This is liability for the mistakes of another; a mistake by Bosman itself is not required."], ["Only if Bosman and the bricklaying firm agreed in advance that Bosman is liable.", "Liability towards third parties follows from the law and does not depend on agreements between the parties."]], "1i.24"],
["Kees Vermeulen runs a shoe shop with four employees in Deventer, in a rented building from 1910. In a strong gust of wind a piece of the weathered façade decoration comes loose. It falls on a passer-by, who goes to hospital with a head wound. The owner of the building lives abroad and has let it to Kees for years. Under the BW, who in principle bears the strict liability for the defective structure?", [["Kees, because he uses the building in the conduct of his business.", "The BW places liability for a defective structure used in a business on the business user; Kees should therefore have this covered under his AVB."], ["Always the owner of the building, even if someone else runs a business in it.", "With business use, liability in principle shifts to whoever runs the business."], ["The municipality, because the passer-by was walking on the public road.", "The municipality is liable for defects in the road, not for a defect in a privately owned building."], ["Nobody, because a gust of wind is an unforeseeable event.", "Strict liability applies to defective structures; the foreseeability of the defect is not decisive."]], "1c.7"],
["How is environmental damage caused by gradual pollution usually dealt with under a standard AVB?", [["Largely excluded; often only a sudden and uncertain event is covered.", "The environmental clause on the AVB limits the cover to acute incidents; for gradual pollution and own premises an environmental liability insurance exists."], ["Fully covered, because environmental damage to neighbours or on neighbouring plots is always damage to third parties.", "Gradual pollution generally falls outside the AVB; a general cover would make the risk unmanageable."], ["Covered, but only if the government issues a remediation order.", "A government order does not make an excluded risk covered; the cover depends on the nature of the event."], ["Covered through the buildings insurance of the business premises.", "A buildings policy covers damage to the building from named events, not the contamination of soil or water."]], "1e.2"],
["Why do many employers take out a collective accident insurance for their employees in addition to their AVB?", [["It pays a fixed amount, regardless of whether the employer is liable.", "An accident insurance prevents the employee from first having to prove liability; that can spare the employment relationship."], ["The AVB does not cover any loss of employees.", "An AVB generally does cover employer's liability; the accident insurance supplements it where that liability is absent."], ["A collective accident insurance is legally compulsory for every employer with staff.", "There is no general legal obligation; sometimes an obligation follows from a collective labour agreement."], ["An accident insurance replaces continued payment of wages during sickness.", "Continued payment of wages remains a legal obligation; an accident benefit is a fixed amount on death or permanent disability."]], "2e.14"],
["Accountant Gerard Lammers has had his own practice with four employees in Gouda for thirty years. His professional liability insurance was taken out on a claims-made basis. On 1 January he stops his practice and considers simply terminating the policy on that date. His adviser points out the risk of claims about old annual accounts. Why is a run-off cover (extended reporting period) important here?", [["Claims about errors from the active period can still be brought after the end of the policy.", "Under claims-made, the moment of the claim and notification is decisive; without run-off cover, a claim brought after termination is not covered."], ["Because a run-off cover also covers new assignments that he still accepts after his retirement.", "Run-off concerns errors from the insured period, not new work."], ["Because the claims-made policy continues to cover all claims about old errors automatically after termination anyway.", "Quite the reverse: without run-off the cover for new claims ends when the policy ends."], ["Because a run-off cover is legally compulsory for every business owner who ends his business.", "There is no general legal obligation; it is a sensible choice given the claims risk."]], "1c.19"],
["A commercial client disagrees with the insurer's rejection of its claim. Which route is as a rule obvious?", [["First complain to the insurer, then possibly to Kifid (Dutch Financial Services Complaints Institute) or to the court.", "The insurer's internal complaints procedure is the first step; after that the client can go to Kifid if it is eligible, or to the court."], ["Straight to the Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM), which decides such a dispute with the insurer in a binding manner.", "The AFM is a supervisor and does not decide individual disputes between client and insurer."], ["Straight to De Nederlandsche Bank (DNB), because it supervises the financial soundness of insurers.", "DNB carries out prudential supervision and does not handle individual complaints about claims."], ["Always to an arbitrator, because the court has no jurisdiction for commercial policies.", "The court remains competent; arbitration applies only if the parties have agreed to it."]], "1i.9"],
["What is the purpose of the care, custody and control clause extension that some AVB policies offer, for example for installers?", [["To co-insure damage to third-party property being worked on, up to a maximum.", "Property in custody is often excluded as standard; an extension offers limited cover for this typical risk of working at clients' premises."], ["To co-insure the installer's own tools and measuring equipment against theft or loss.", "Own tools are a property risk that belongs on a separate goods or tools policy."], ["To compensate the repair of its own poorly executed work, as a rule in full amount.", "The costs of redoing one's own defective work are a business risk and remain excluded."], ["To cover liability for advisory errors in design assignments for the clients.", "Advisory errors with pure financial loss belong under a professional liability insurance."]], "1c.17"],
["A large group buys its liability cover in layers: a primary policy up to a certain amount and above that one or more excess policies. When does an excess insurer pay?", [["Only when the loss exceeds the amount of the underlying layer or layers.", "An excess layer connects to the underlying cover and pays only the part above it, up to its own limit."], ["For every loss, in proportion to its share in the total cover that has been bought.", "That is characteristic of co-insurance; with layers, a higher layer pays only once the lower one is exhausted."], ["Only if the primary insurer is bankrupt and can no longer pay its share of the loss.", "An excess layer is not a replacement for a bankrupt insurer but a supplement above a certain amount."], ["Only if the loss is the result of intent on the part of the group concerned.", "Intent is generally excluded; the layer in which a loss falls depends on its size."]], "2e.9"],
["Installation company Wolters in Hengelo has 25 fitters, each with a van. A fitter momentarily fails to pay attention when merging and drives into the back of a stationary car. The comprehensive damage to the van is €3,500, of which €1,000 is for the employer's excess / deductible. The director wants to deduct that excess / deductible from the fitter's wages. Can the employer do so under the BW?", [["In principle not; only in the case of intent or deliberate recklessness by the employee.", "The BW protects the employee: loss that he causes the employer at work is in principle for the employer's account, except in the case of intent or deliberate recklessness."], ["Yes, because the employee caused the accident through his own inattention while driving.", "Ordinary inattention is insufficient; intent or deliberate recklessness is required."], ["Yes, because damage to company cars falls outside the protective rule of the BW altogether.", "The rule in fact also applies to damage to the employer's property, such as company cars."], ["Only if the employee has been employed for less than a year.", "The length of employment plays no role in this rule."]], "1g.3"],
["Supplier Van Asten in Helmond, with ninety employees, makes brake components for a German car manufacturer. Because of an error in the production process, a series of components turns out to be too weak. The car manufacturer recalls thousands of cars to replace the components and holds the supplier liable for all costs. No accidents have happened yet. Which costs are usually not covered under the supplier's AVB?", [["The costs of the recall and of replacing its own defective components.", "The insured's own product and the recall costs are generally excluded under an AVB; for recall costs a separate recall insurance exists."], ["Personal injury to motorists if they are later involved in an accident after all because of the defect.", "Personal injury of third parties caused by a defective product is in fact a core cover of the AVB."], ["Damage to other parts of the car caused by the breaking of the brake component.", "Consequential damage to other property of third parties is generally covered under the AVB."], ["The costs of legal defence against an unjustified claim by an injured motorist.", "Defence costs are generally within the cover of an AVB."]], "1e.2"],
["Patrick Willems is the sole director of Willems Interieurbouw bv in Venray, a company with nine employees. The bv has large payment arrears and the bank has terminated the credit. Even so, Patrick orders €70,000 of board material on behalf of the bv, while he knows that the bv cannot pay for it and offers no recourse. Three months later the supplier has still not been paid. What is correct?", [["Patrick can be personally liable in tort.", "According to established case law, a director acts wrongfully if he enters into obligations on behalf of the company that he knows or ought to know will not be fulfilled."], ["Only the bv is liable, because a director is never personally liable.", "The starting point is that the company is liable, but where there is a serious personal reproach the director can also be held liable."], ["Patrick is only liable if the bv goes bankrupt within a year.", "Bankruptcy is not a condition for a director's liability towards a creditor."], ["The supplier must first claim against the shareholders of the bv.", "Shareholders of a bv are in principle not liable for the company's debts."]], "1g.3"],
["The board of a bv consists of three persons. One director disagreed with a decision that led to improper management and tried to prevent the consequences. What is correct as regards liability towards the bv?", [["He can exculpate himself if no serious reproach can be made against him and he was not negligent.", "Directors are collectively responsible, but a director against whom no serious reproach can be made and who was not negligent in averting the consequences can free himself."], ["He is always jointly and severally liable for the whole, regardless of his own stance on the board.", "Collective responsibility allows for exculpation by the director against whom no serious reproach can be made."], ["Only the chair of the board can be liable.", "All directors bear responsibility for the management, not only the chair."], ["He is free of liability as long as he has not signed himself.", "Signing is not decisive; what matters is the culpability of his role in the board."]], "1g.3"],
["Construction company Pronk bv in Lelystad has major liquidity problems because of a few loss-making projects. The payroll taxes for the past two months cannot be paid. Director Erik Pronk hopes that a new project will quickly bring in money and waits to act. His adviser points out the risk of personal liability. What must the board do to reduce the chance of director's liability towards the Dutch Tax and Customs Administration (Belastingdienst)?", [["Report the inability to pay to the Belastingdienst in good time.", "After a timely report, the Belastingdienst must show that the non-payment is due to manifestly improper management; without a report this is presumed."], ["Wait until the new project brings in money and then pay everything at once.", "Without a timely report it is presumed that the non-payment is attributable to the director."], ["Take out a D&O insurance, which always takes over the bv's tax debts.", "A D&O policy does not cover the bv's own tax debts and often has restrictions for this liability."], ["Convert the bv immediately into a vof, so that the debt lands with the partners.", "That solves nothing and in fact makes the entrepreneurs' position worse."]], "1c.17"],
["A D&O policy has a 'side B' cover. What does this element cover?", [["Reimbursement to the company of what it paid to the director under an indemnification.", "If the company indemnifies its director and pays, side B reimburses that expenditure to the company."], ["Direct cover for the director himself, in case the company cannot or will not indemnify him.", "That is side A: the director is covered directly if the company does not pay or cannot pay."], ["The company's own liability as a legal entity.", "That is entity cover (side C), which is often limited to certain claims."], ["Shareholders' loss from a fall in the share price.", "Shareholder claims can lead to a claim, but side B concerns reimbursement to the company."]], "1c.4"],
["A bv is taken over and the directors step down. Why is a run-off cover on the D&O policy often arranged on the takeover?", [["Claims about decisions from before the takeover may still come later, while the old policy ends.", "A D&O policy usually works on a claims-made basis; without run-off, later claims about the old board are not covered."], ["Run-off means the new owner no longer needs to take out its own D&O policy for the new board.", "Run-off concerns the past; the new board needs its own cover."], ["Run-off is mandatory in order to register the takeover with the Chamber of Commerce.", "Registration with the Chamber of Commerce does not depend on an insurance."], ["Run-off compensates the purchase price if the buyer feels deceived.", "A dispute about the purchase price is not directors' liability and is dealt with elsewhere."]], "1c.19"],
["Why can a D&O insurance also be relevant for directors of a foundation or association?", [["They too can be held personally liable for improper management.", "Since that legislation, comparable liability rules apply to directors and supervisors of associations and foundations as for bv's and nv's."], ["Directors of foundations are legally required to have a D&O insurance.", "There is no general legal obligation; it is a choice, though sometimes required by grant providers."], ["A foundation has no capital of its own, so the directors always answer for all debts.", "A foundation is a legal entity that answers for its own debts; directors are only liable where there is a personal reproach."], ["D&O insurance covers the foundation's operating deficits.", "Operating deficits are a business risk and not loss caused by a claim against a director."]], "1c.12"],
["A contractor must remedy defects during a maintenance period after completion. What loss does a CAR policy usually cover during that period?", [["Loss the contractor causes during maintenance work and loss with a cause from the construction period.", "After completion the CAR cover continues in limited form; it then no longer covers all damage to the works, but it does cover these specific situations."], ["All damage to the completed works, also through storm or fire during use by the client of the works.", "After completion, use is at the client's risk; fire and storm then belong under its buildings policy."], ["Only the costs of regular maintenance work such as painting, which the contractor carries out.", "Normal maintenance is not an uncertain event and therefore not insured."], ["No loss at all, because the CAR cover ends at completion of the works by the contractor.", "Many CAR policies have a run-on cover for the maintenance period."]], "1e.1"],
["During the renovation of an existing office building, the existing part, which is not being renovated, is damaged by the works. Which CAR section is intended for this?", [["The section for existing property of the client.", "In a renovation and extension, the existing building is not 'works in progress'; a separate section or extension exists for it."], ["The section for works in progress.", "That section covers the work to be newly built or renovated, not the existing parts outside it."], ["The section for construction plant and tools.", "Plant concerns the contractor's machines and tools, not the client's building."], ["The section for the costs of extra security of the building site.", "Security is a preventive measure, not cover for damage to existing property."]], "1c.3"],
["A contractor carries out dozens of similar projects of limited size every year. Which form of CAR fits best?", [["A continuous CAR policy that automatically covers projects within defined limits.", "This avoids having to take out a separate policy for each project and each project is covered from the start."], ["A separate project policy for each project, taken out after construction has started.", "A separate policy per project is laborious; moreover a gap arises if it is only taken out after the start."], ["No CAR, because small projects fall under the AVB.", "The AVB covers liability, not damage to the insured's own works in progress."], ["A professional liability insurance for all projects.", "That covers pure financial loss from design or advisory errors, not physical damage to the works."]], "1c.12"],
["Why is heavy construction plant, such as a crane or excavator, often not insured on the CAR policy but on a separate plant insurance?", [["The plant goes from project to project, whereas a project CAR is tied to one set of works.", "A plant policy covers the machines continuously, wherever they are; that fits their use better."], ["Plant cannot be insured in any way.", "Plant is insurable, for example through a plant insurance on a comprehensive-type basis."], ["The CAR policy covers only construction plant that is owned by the client of the works.", "Plant of the client does not automatically fall under the CAR either; the division follows from the nature of the object."], ["A crane must always be insured on the WAM policy.", "The WAM only comes into play if the plant takes part in traffic as a motor vehicle; that says nothing about damage to the machine itself."]], "1c.9"],
["A housing association decides to take out the CAR insurance for a large new-build project itself instead of the contractor. What is an important advantage of this?", [["The association keeps control over cover, sums insured and conditions.", "As client it can ensure that the whole project is properly covered and that no gaps or duplications arise between the parties' policies."], ["The contractor then no longer has to bear any liability for its work.", "A CAR policy does not remove the contractor's contractual obligations."], ["The association can thereby deduct the construction costs for tax purposes.", "Who takes out the CAR has no influence on the tax treatment of the construction costs."], ["The association then no longer has to take out a buildings insurance after completion.", "After completion the CAR cover largely ends; the building must then be insured separately."]], "2e.4"],
["Earthmoving company De Vries in Drachten, with twenty employees, lays sewers for municipalities. The CAR policy states that an increased excess / deductible applies for damage to cables and pipes if no excavation notification was made beforehand to the Kadaster (KLIC). A new site manager wants to skip that notification because, in his view, 'there are never any cables there anyway'. While digging, the excavator hits a fibre-optic cable. What is the purpose of such a clause?", [["To encourage the contractor to request the location of cables and pipes beforehand.", "An excavation notification is a legal obligation and an important preventive measure; the increased excess / deductible makes negligence financially noticeable."], ["To lower the premium for contractors who never work near cables.", "The clause concerns behaviour on every excavation job, not a premium discount."], ["To exclude damage to cables entirely, even if the excavation notification was made.", "With a timely notification the normal excess / deductible in fact applies; there is no full exclusion."], ["To shift liability for cable damage to the owner of the cable.", "A policy clause does not change liability towards the cable owner."]], "1c.6"],
["A ship gets into distress in heavy weather and the captain has part of the cargo thrown overboard in order to save the ship and the remaining cargo. What is the arrangement called under which this sacrifice is divided among all interested parties?", [["General average (avarij-grosse).", "Under general average, ship and all cargo interests contribute in proportion to sacrifices made for the common preservation; the cargo insurance covers that contribution."], ["Particular average.", "Particular average is damage that affects only a certain cargo or the ship and is borne by the interested party itself."], ["Subrogation.", "Subrogation is the transfer of the claim against a liable third party to the insurer after payment."], ["Abandonment.", "Abandonment is the surrender of an insured object to the insurer; that is a different arrangement."]], "1c.13"],
["An exporter can choose between the Institute Cargo Clauses A, B and C. Which clauses offer the widest cover for the cargo?", [["Clauses A, which offer all-risks cover subject to the exclusions.", "The A clauses cover all risks of loss or damage that are not expressly excluded; B and C cover only named perils."], ["Clauses C, because they remove the most exclusions.", "The C clauses are in fact the most limited and cover only a limited number of named perils, such as fire and stranding."], ["Clauses B, because they lie between A and C and therefore cover everything.", "B lies between A and C in scope, but still covers only named perils."], ["All three are equal; the difference lies only in the premium.", "There is a substantial difference in the scope of cover; that also explains the premium difference."]], "1c.15"],
["In an international road transport to which the CMR Convention applies, no consignment note has been drawn up. What is the consequence?", [["The contract of carriage remains valid and the CMR Convention remains applicable.", "The consignment note is a means of evidence; its absence does not affect the existence and validity of the contract of carriage."], ["The contract of carriage is void, so that no liability of the carrier exists at all.", "The convention expressly provides that the contract is valid even without a consignment note."], ["The CMR no longer applies and national law applies in full to the carriage concerned.", "Applicability depends on the international nature of the carriage, not on the consignment note."], ["The carrier becomes liable without any limit for any loss that occurs during the carriage.", "The absence of a consignment note does not in itself lead to unlimited liability."]], "1c.13"],
["In which case is a CMR carrier not liable for damage to the cargo?", [["If the damage results from an inherent defect of the goods or unavoidable circumstances.", "The convention contains grounds for exemption, including an inherent defect of the goods and unavoidable circumstances with unpreventable consequences."], ["If it engaged another carrier for the transport and that carrier caused the damage.", "The carrier is also liable for the persons whose services it uses."], ["If its lorry was older than ten years at the time of the carriage in question.", "The age of the vehicle is not a ground for exemption; defects of the vehicle are in fact for the carrier's account."], ["If the sender has not taken out a transport insurance for the goods carried.", "Whether the cargo is insured does not change the carrier's liability."]], "1c.13"],
["A haulage company transports goods from Rotterdam to Groningen. Which regime in principle determines the carrier's liability?", [["Book 8 of the BW, in practice often supplemented by the General Transport Conditions (AVC).", "The CMR Convention does not apply to domestic road carriage; the BW and the standard conditions then regulate liability."], ["The CMR Convention, because it applies to all road carriage anywhere in Europe.", "The CMR applies only to international road carriage between different countries."], ["Exclusively the general rules on tort, because no special rules for carriage exist at all.", "There is a contract of carriage, to which specific rules on carriage apply."], ["There is no statutory regulation; the parties must agree everything themselves.", "Book 8 of the BW does contain an extensive regime for road carriage."]], "1c.13"],
["Electronics wholesaler Nguyen in Tilburg has 22 employees and receives a pallet of monitors from Poland. The carriage falls under the CMR Convention. The driver delivers on Friday afternoon; the packaging looks undamaged and the consignment note is signed without remarks. Only when the boxes are unpacked a few days later does it turn out that twenty monitors are broken. What must the wholesaler do to protect its position?", [["Make a written reservation within seven days of delivery, Sundays and public holidays not counted.", "For non-apparent damage the convention provides this period; without a timely reservation the goods are presumed to have been received in good condition."], ["Nothing; the wholesaler can hold the carrier liable at any time until limitation without further steps.", "Without a timely reservation it is assumed that the goods were delivered in good condition, which makes proof much harder."], ["Start proceedings within a year, because a reservation can no longer be made after the consignment note has been signed.", "Even for non-apparent damage a later written reservation is possible; the limitation period is something different."], ["Have the broken monitors destroyed immediately to prevent further damage and storage costs.", "Destroying the goods makes proving the damage impossible and is not a salvage measure."]], "1i.4"],
["A trading company sends consignments to customers at home and abroad every week. Which transport insurance fits best?", [["An open (floating) policy that automatically covers all consignments.", "With an open policy every consignment is covered without needing a policy each time; the premium follows from the declared values."], ["A separate policy for each consignment, requested after dispatch.", "Requesting a policy after dispatch is too late if the loss has already occurred in transit; moreover it is laborious."], ["No insurance, because the carrier is always liable for the full value.", "Carriers' liability is often limited and subject to grounds for exemption; the cargo itself remains uninsured."], ["A contents insurance with cover away from the premises.", "A contents policy with cover away from the premises is not intended for trade consignments in transit."]], "1c.15"],
["Machine builder Van der Meer in Heerenveen sells a packaging machine to a buyer in Germany. The contract is concluded on the basis of the Incoterm EXW (ex works). The German buyer has the machine collected by its own carrier. On the way the lorry tips over and the machine is badly damaged. Who has the main interest in a transport insurance during the carriage?", [["The buyer, because the risk passes as soon as the machine is ready at the seller's premises.", "Under EXW the seller has fulfilled its obligations once the goods are made available at its premises; the transport risk lies with the buyer."], ["The seller, because it remains owner until the machine has been delivered in Germany.", "Under EXW the risk in fact already passes from the seller to the buyer."], ["The carrier, which is the only one with an insurable interest in the cargo.", "The carrier insures its liability; the interest in the cargo itself lies with the buyer."], ["Seller and buyer each half, because EXW divides the risk.", "A shared risk is not a feature of EXW."]], "1c.16"],
["Under the WAM, who is obliged to have a motor vehicle insured?", [["The owner or keeper, in practice usually the registration holder.", "The law places the insurance obligation on the owner or keeper; the RDW (Netherlands Vehicle Authority) checks that obligation on the basis of the registration number."], ["Exclusively the driver who is driving at that moment.", "The driver is co-insured, but the insurance obligation rests on the owner or keeper."], ["The employer of every employee who drives a motor vehicle for business.", "An employer is only subject to the insurance obligation if it is the owner or keeper of the vehicle."], ["The supplier who sold the vehicle.", "After the sale, the supplier is no longer owner or keeper and does not bear that obligation."]], "1g.1"],
["Installation company Dekker in Purmerend has ten vans, each fitted out with tools and materials worth about €8,000. At night, a van parked in front of a fitter's house in a residential area is broken into. All the tools and materials are stolen; the van itself has only a broken window. The vans are insured on all-risks comprehensive terms. Is the stolen content usually covered under the comprehensive insurance?", [["Usually not; tools and cargo must be insured separately.", "The comprehensive insurance covers the vehicle itself and often fixed accessories; loose tools and cargo fall outside it in most policies."], ["Yes, because all-risks comprehensive covers everything that is in the van.", "All-risks concerns damage to the vehicle, not the contents."], ["Yes, provided the van was locked and there are signs of forced entry.", "Signs of forced entry are relevant for a tools or goods cover, but do not make the contents comprehensively insured."], ["No, and this risk cannot be insured separately anywhere either.", "Tools and cargo in vans can be insured separately, often with conditions."]], "1c.9"],
["Advisory firm Groen & Water in Wageningen has fifteen advisers and four pool cars on a fleet policy. Adviser Ilse Bos may borrow one of those cars at the weekend for a house move. On Saturday, while reversing into a parking space, she hits a parked car belonging to a local resident. The director wonders whether the private use causes problems. Is the resident's damage covered under the WAM cover of the fleet policy?", [["Yes, WAM cover applies to every driver who drives with permission, also for private use.", "The WAM protects road accident victims; the insured liability is not limited to business journeys."], ["No, the WAM cover on a fleet policy applies exclusively to business journeys of the company.", "The WAM cover is linked to the motor vehicle and its drivers, not to the purpose of the journey."], ["No, Ilse must herself have a WAM insurance for private journeys in a pool car of the firm.", "There need not be a second WAM insurance; the vehicle is already insured."], ["Only if Ilse herself is not at fault for the collision with the parked car of the resident.", "Precisely when the driver is liable, the WAM policy covers the other party's damage."]], "1g.1"],
["What is the function of the green card (international motor insurance certificate) for a fleet that drives abroad?", [["It is an internationally recognised proof that a valid motor liability insurance exists.", "The green card shows in the participating countries that the vehicle is insured; within the EU the registration plate in principle counts as proof."], ["It is an additional comprehensive cover for damage to the company's own car while abroad.", "The green card says nothing about comprehensive cover; it proves the liability cover."], ["It is a licence that entitles the holder to carry goods abroad on a professional basis.", "Other licences apply to professional goods transport; the green card is proof of insurance."], ["It is an environmental label that gives access to foreign low-emission zones in cities.", "Low-emission zones have their own registrations and stickers; the green card is unrelated."]], "1g.1"],
["Which element of a cyber insurance often proves most valuable to an SME in a ransomware attack?", [["Immediate incident response by IT forensic, legal and communications specialists.", "Rapid expert help limits the standstill and loss; many SMEs do not have that knowledge in-house."], ["Compensation for the depreciation of the computers.", "The equipment often remains intact in ransomware; the problem lies in the encrypted data and the standstill."], ["Cover for theft of employees' laptops and phones from a parked company car.", "Theft of hardware is a property risk and not part of the core of a cyber incident."], ["The premium discount on reporting the incident.", "Reporting earns no premium discount; the value lies in the help and compensation after the incident."]], "1c.4"],
["Customers hold an online shop liable for loss after their data was leaked in a hack. Why does the AVB usually offer no cover for this?", [["The AVB mainly covers injury and property damage; this is financial loss from a data breach.", "Data is not regarded as property and a data breach leads to financial loss; therefore a cyber insurance with liability cover is needed for this risk."], ["The AVB never covers loss of the company's customers, only loss of chance passers-by on the premises.", "Customers are also third parties; the problem is the nature of the loss, not who the injured party is."], ["The AVB only pays if the online shop carried out the hack itself on its own initiative.", "Intent of the insured is in fact excluded; that does not explain the missing cover."], ["A data breach always falls under the legal expenses insurance of the customer concerned.", "The customer's legal expenses cover helps it with recourse, but does not cover the online shop's liability."]], "1c.9"],
["How do cyber policies usually deal with fines that a supervisor such as the Dutch Data Protection Authority (Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens) imposes after a data breach?", [["They are often excluded or covered only to the extent they are legally insurable.", "The insurability of administrative fines is limited; a fine is meant to hit the offender itself."], ["They are always fully compensated, because they are the direct consequence of a covered incident.", "A covered incident does not automatically make a fine insurable; policies restrict this."], ["They are compensated by the Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens itself if the company cooperates.", "A supervisor does not compensate fines; cooperation can at most influence the amount."], ["They fall under the company's own D&O cover.", "D&O covers claims against directors; fines on the company are not covered by it."]], "1e.2"],
["Accountancy firm Brink in Hilversum has 45 employees and works entirely in the cloud through an external software supplier. That supplier is hit by a ransomware attack, as a result of which the firm cannot access its files for two days. The firm's own systems were not hacked. The firm loses turnover and has to pay overtime to catch up. What is important for the cover under the firm's cyber policy?", [["Whether the policy also covers business interruption caused by failure at an external IT service provider.", "Many cyber policies cover standstill due to an incident at the insured itself; failure at a service provider often requires an extension."], ["Nothing, because a cyber policy always covers any disruption in IT systems, regardless of where it arises.", "That is not so; the cover for failure at third parties differs greatly per policy."], ["Whether the firm's employees have taken a course on phishing.", "Awareness is useful prevention, but does not determine here whether failure at a supplier is covered."], ["Whether the firm can hold the cloud supplier liable on the basis of product liability.", "Product liability concerns defective products that cause injury or property damage; that is not at issue here."]], "1e.2"],
["A van belonging to an installation company is hit by a car whose driver turns out to be uninsured. To which body can the company turn for the loss?", [["The Motor Traffic Guarantee Fund (Waarborgfonds Motorverkeer).", "The Guarantee Fund compensates, under conditions, damage caused by an uninsured motor vehicle."], ["Kifid (Dutch Financial Services Complaints Institute).", "Kifid handles complaints about financial service providers, not damage caused by uninsured motorists."], ["The Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM).", "The AFM supervises the conduct of financial firms and does not compensate loss."], ["The Violent Offences Compensation Fund (Schadefonds Geweldsmisdrijven).", "That fund is for victims of violent crimes, not for traffic damage."]], "1i.20"],
["A contractor includes in a contract with a client that it is liable for all loss, even if it would not be liable by law. How do most AVB policies deal with this?", [["They cover only statutory liability; a contractual extension of it is excluded.", "An insurer does not want the insured to enlarge its risk unilaterally; liability that goes beyond the law is generally outside the cover."], ["They always follow the contractual agreements, because the contract takes precedence over the policy.", "The contract binds the contractor and its client, not the insurer; the latter covers according to the policy conditions."], ["They cover the extension, provided the contractor reports the contract to the insurer within a year.", "A report does not simply make an excluded contractual liability covered."], ["They lapse entirely, because the contractor increased its risk without permission.", "The cover for statutory liability remains; only the excess falls outside it."]], "1c.18"],
["What is the waiting period that many commercial legal expenses insurances have?", [["A period after inception during which there is not yet cover for certain disputes.", "The waiting period prevents someone from taking out an insurance as soon as it sees a conflict coming, even if that was not yet foreseeable."], ["The time the insurer has to engage a lawyer.", "That is a handling period; the waiting period concerns the moment from which cover exists."], ["The period during which a judgment cannot yet be enforced.", "That is a procedural law matter and not part of the policy."], ["The period after the end of the policy during which loss can still be reported to the insurer.", "That is a run-off period; the waiting period lies at the start of the insurance."]], "2e.10"],
["A wholesaler mainly wants to use a legal expenses insurance to collect unpaid invoices on which the customer has no substantive dispute. What is correct?", [["Collection of undisputed claims is generally not covered, or only to a limited extent.", "Legal expenses insurance is intended for legal disputes; for collecting undisputed invoices a debt collection service or credit insurance is more suitable."], ["Collection is always fully covered, because it concerns a legal claim.", "Undisputed claims are in fact often excluded or only limitedly covered."], ["Collection is covered, but only if the invoice is higher than the AVB's excess / deductible.", "The excess / deductible of another policy has no influence on the cover of the legal expenses policy."], ["Collection is covered if the customer is established abroad.", "The debtor's place of establishment does not change the nature of an undisputed claim."]], "2e.10"],
["Haulage company Kok in Barendrecht has fifty lorries and a commercial legal expenses insurance. A major client has terminated the contract early and Kok wants to claim €250,000 in damages. The legal expenses insurer's lawyer considers the case hopeless and does not want to litigate. Director Arjen Kok completely disagrees. What do most legal expenses policies offer in that situation?", [["A dispute procedure: an external lawyer gives an opinion at the insurer's expense.", "This lets the insured obtain an independent opinion on the prospects; if the insurer follows that opinion, handling continues."], ["No possibility at all; the insurer's opinion on the prospects is always binding.", "Policies in fact offer an arrangement for this kind of disagreement, among other things to prevent conflicts of interest."], ["An automatic payment of half the financial interest of the dispute.", "A buy-out of the interest is a different arrangement; here the issue is a difference of opinion on the approach."], ["A mandatory referral to the Enterprise Chamber for a binding ruling.", "The Enterprise Chamber handles company law disputes and is not intended for disagreements with an insurer."]], "2e.10"],
["A contractor's customer holds the contractor liable for damage to his home. Which of the contractor's policies is in the first instance the one to conduct the defence?", [["The AVB, because the liability insurer also conducts the defence.", "A liability insurer assesses the claim, conducts the defence and pays if the claim is justified."], ["The legal expenses insurance, because every legal conflict falls under it.", "Defence against a liability claim covered under the AVB belongs to the liability insurer; legal expenses insurance often excludes this."], ["The customer's CAR insurance.", "The customer has no CAR policy for the contractor's work, and it would not conduct the contractor's defence either."], ["The contractor's buildings insurance.", "A buildings policy covers damage to the contractor's own building, not liability."]], "1c.9"],
["A business owner is suspected of an intentional offence and asks for legal assistance in his criminal case. How do many legal expenses policies deal with this?", [["Cover often follows only afterwards, if the insured is acquitted.", "Insurers do not want to finance intent; if there is an acquittal or discharge from prosecution, the costs are compensated after all."], ["The costs are always covered in full in advance, regardless of the outcome.", "For intentional offences, cover in advance is usually excluded; the outcome of the case is decisive."], ["The insurer also pays any fine if the business owner is convicted.", "Criminal fines are not insurable; they must hit the offender himself."], ["Criminal cases can never fall under a legal expenses insurance.", "Many policies do offer criminal legal assistance, albeit with limitations in the case of intent."]], "2e.10"],
["With an insurance concluded through an authority holder (gevolmachtigd agent, a party with binding authority), who bears the insurance risk?", [["The insurer that granted the authority.", "The authority holder acts on behalf of the insurer; the risk and the obligation to pay out lie with that insurer."], ["The authority holder itself, because it accepted the risk.", "Accepting on behalf of another does not make the authority holder a risk carrier."], ["The adviser who referred the client to the authority holder.", "An adviser bears no insurance risk; it can be liable for an advisory error."], ["The policyholder, until the insurer has approved the policy.", "After acceptance by the authority holder, the insurer is bound; no further approval is needed."]], "1f.1"],
["Does an authority holder (gevolmachtigd agent) that accepts commercial non-life insurances need a licence from the Netherlands Authority for the Financial Markets (AFM)?", [["Yes, acting as an authority holder requires a licence under the Financial Supervision Act (Wft).", "The Wft counts acting as an authority holder among the financial services that require a licence."], ["No, because the insurer already has a licence from De Nederlandsche Bank (DNB) to operate.", "The insurer's licence does not cover the authority holder's own services."], ["No, as long as the authority holder serves only business clients and no consumers at all.", "The licence requirement in principle also applies to services to business clients."], ["Only if the authority holder also handles claims for the insurer on its behalf.", "The licence requirement already follows from accepting on behalf of the insurer."]], "1i.6"],
["An authority holder accepts a risk that falls outside the limits of its authority. The policyholder did not know this and was entitled to rely on the acceptance. What is correct?", [["The insurer may be bound through justified reliance and may hold the authority holder liable internally.", "In the case of apparent authority the third party is protected; exceeding the authority is a matter between insurer and authority holder."], ["The insurance is always void, because the authority holder was not authorised.", "The BW protects a third party who could reasonably rely on the authority."], ["The authority holder itself becomes the insurer of the risk.", "An authority holder is not an insurer; it can at most be liable towards the insurer or the client."], ["The policyholder must bear the loss itself, because it should have checked the limits of the authority.", "A policyholder does not have to know the internal arrangements between insurer and authority holder."]], "1f.1"],
["Within its authority, an authority holder develops its own package policy for bakeries and determines the cover and conditions itself. Which IDD rules are relevant to it?", [["The rules on product approval and review, because it is also a product developer.", "Whoever determines essential features of a product counts as a (co-)developer and must, among other things, determine a target market and test the product."], ["None, because the IDD applies only to life insurance.", "The IDD applies to all insurance distribution, so also to non-life insurance."], ["Only the rules on the professional competence of its staff.", "Professional competence also applies, but with its own product development the product rules come clearly into play."], ["Only the rules that apply to insurers with a licence from De Nederlandsche Bank.", "An authority holder does not have a licence as an insurer; the product rules apply to it as a developer."]], "2e.4"],
["Engineering firm Delta Dijkwerk in Rotterdam has 80 employees. Project manager Joost van Wijk is going to live in Saudi Arabia with his family for three years to lead a harbour project. He remains employed by the Dutch firm. The employer has a collective travel insurance and wants to know what has to be arranged for Joost. What should the adviser above all look at?", [["Check whether Joost remains insured under the Health Insurance Act (Zvw); otherwise expat cover is needed.", "Someone who goes to live and work abroad may fall outside Dutch health insurance; an expat policy fills that gap."], ["Nothing, because his Dutch health insurance always applies as long as he works for a Dutch employer.", "Whether he remains insured depends on the rules for working abroad; that is not always the case."], ["The collective travel insurance fully covers a stay abroad of three years, including his family.", "A travel insurance is intended for temporary trips and does not cover long-term living and working abroad."], ["A collective accident insurance covers all his medical costs abroad, whatever the cause.", "An accident insurance pays fixed amounts on accidents and does not replace a health insurance."]], "1e.27"],
["Bakery Ramdin in The Hague delivers bread to restaurants with three vans. A delivery driver is driving calmly along a residential street when a twelve-year-old boy suddenly cycles into the road between parked cars. The van cannot stop in time and the boy breaks his arm. Witness statements show that the boy did not look out. What is in principle the consequence for the compensation?", [["The bakery's WAM insurer in principle compensates the boy's full loss.", "For children under 14, established case law on Article 185 of the Road Traffic Act (WVW) in principle gives full compensation, even if the child made a mistake."], ["The boy receives nothing, because he did not look out himself and the driver made no mistake.", "In collisions with vulnerable road users, the owner or keeper of the motor vehicle is subject to strict liability."], ["The boy in principle receives half, because he is partly to blame for the accident.", "The 50% rule applies to cyclists and pedestrians of 14 years and older; for younger children it is different."], ["The boy's parents must compensate the damage to the van, and the boy himself receives nothing.", "The parents may be liable for damage caused by their child, but that does not remove the obligation to compensate the injury."]], "1i.21"],
["Why does an insurer itself take out reinsurance?", [["To limit its own retention per risk or event and to be able to offer more capacity.", "By passing on part of large risks, the insurer can accept large policies without a single loss threatening its capital."], ["To give the policyholder a direct claim against a second insurer.", "In reinsurance the policyholder in principle has no claim against the reinsurer."], ["To be able to guarantee a lower premium to the policyholder.", "Reinsurance also costs the insurer premium; a lower premium for the client is not a fixed consequence."], ["Because the law prohibits an insurer from bearing more than one risk per sector.", "No such legal prohibition exists; reinsurance is a risk management choice."]], "1f.1"],
["In which situation is an expert valuation most useful for a business client?", [["For a listed building or an art collection, whose value is hard to determine.", "A valuation fixes the value in advance and prevents discussion about value and underinsurance after a loss."], ["For a new van, whose purchase invoice and list value are known to everyone involved.", "The value of a new van is easy to determine; a valuation adds little."], ["For stock that changes daily and is declared to the insurer on a monthly basis.", "For changing stock a declaration arrangement is more obvious than a fixed valuation."], ["For new office contents purchased last year, of which the invoices are available.", "The value is evident from the invoices; a valuation is then rarely needed."]], "1h.4"],
["Sanitary wholesaler Verbruggen in Waalwijk supplies on account to more than four hundred installers and contractors. The payment term is thirty days and the largest customers sometimes have €150,000 outstanding. Last year two customers went bankrupt, which cost the company €90,000. The financial director wants to manage this risk without losing customers. Which insurance fits best?", [["A credit insurance, under which the insurer sets a credit limit per customer.", "The credit insurer assesses customers' creditworthiness and sets limits; the policy thus helps both to prevent and to compensate loss."], ["A commercial legal expenses insurance that compensates unpaid invoices in bankruptcy.", "Legal expenses insurance helps with disputes, but does not compensate uncollectable claims on a bankrupt customer."], ["A business interruption insurance, because loss of turnover through non-payment falls under it.", "Business interruption requires covered material damage, such as fire; non-payment does not fall under it."], ["A business liability insurance with extended cover for financial loss.", "An AVB covers liability towards others, not the company's own risk of non-payment."]], "1e.22"],
["A bv does not pay its AVB premium on the due date. From when, under the BW, is there in principle no longer any cover?", [["If payment has not been made within fourteen days of a reminder sent after the due date.", "The insurer must first send a reminder and state the consequences; only if payment is not made in time after that can the cover be suspended."], ["Immediately on the due date itself, without a reminder from the insurer being needed.", "For suspension of cover, a reminder after the due date is in principle required first."], ["Only when the insurer has terminated the insurance in writing and has stated its reasons.", "Suspension of cover after a reminder is different from termination; the insurance can continue while the cover is suspended."], ["After three months, regardless of whether the insurer has sent a reminder to the policyholder.", "A fixed period of three months without a reminder does not exist in the law."]], "1i.11"],
["Within what period does a legal claim for payment under a non-life insurance become time-barred in principle under the BW?", [["Three years from the day after the entitled person learned that the claim was due.", "The law has its own limitation period for payment claims under insurance, which can be interrupted by a written claim."], ["Five years from the day the loss arose, as with other compensation for damage in general.", "Five years is the general period for claims for damages; for payments under insurance there is a separate regime."], ["One year after the date of loss, as with claims under the CMR Convention for carriage by road.", "The CMR period applies to claims arising from international road carriage, not to payments under insurance."], ["Twenty years after the insurance was taken out by the policyholder concerned.", "A twenty-year period appears in the BW as an absolute period for compensation of damage, not for insurance payments."]], "1g.3"],
["Restaurant De Gouden Lepel in Maastricht has twelve employees and a contents and buildings policy. In January owner Thijs Coenen sees a damp patch behind the bar, but is busy and reports nothing. Only in April, when the floor begins to rot, does he report the water damage to the insurer. Because of the late report the expert can no longer properly establish the cause, and the damage has moreover become larger. What is in principle the consequence under the BW?", [["The insurer can reduce the payment by the loss it suffers through the late report.", "The BW requires notification as soon as reasonably possible; if this is breached, the insurer can reduce the payment by the prejudice it thereby suffers."], ["The insurer may always refuse payment entirely if a loss is reported later than a month.", "Full forfeiture is only possible if the policy so provides and the insurer's reasonable interests have been harmed."], ["There are no consequences, because a duty to report is not in the law but only in the policy.", "The law does contain a duty to report, with a possible reduction of the payment as the consequence."], ["The insurer must pay in full, but may increase the premium for the following year.", "The statutory consequence concerns the payment, not a premium increase afterwards."]], "1i.12"],
["Clothing shop Mode by Nadia in Almere is broken into at night. Clothing and a till worth about €9,000 are stolen. Owner Nadia Bakker also puts on the loss list a batch of leather jackets and two laptops that were never there, to raise the claim to €21,000. The expert discovers that the jackets were never purchased. What is in principle the consequence?", [["The right to payment lapses entirely, unless the deception does not justify that forfeiture.", "In the case of intent to mislead the insurer, any right to payment lapses; in addition the insurer can terminate the policy and register the fraud."], ["Only the inflated part of the claim is not paid; the real loss is compensated in full.", "In the case of intentional deception the law lets the entire right lapse, not only the exaggerated part."], ["The insurer may only increase the premium for the next insurance year, nothing more.", "Intentional deception has more far-reaching consequences than a premium increase."], ["There is no consequence, as long as the burglary itself really took place at the premises.", "Even if the event is real, deliberately exaggerating the loss leads to forfeiture of the right."]], "4a.1"],
["A business owner sets fire to his own shed in order to collect the insurance payment. What does the BW provide about this loss?", [["The insurer does not pay for loss that the insured caused intentionally or recklessly.", "Insurance is intended for uncertain events; loss caused intentionally or recklessly falls outside it."], ["The insurer pays, but may later recover the amount from the fire brigade.", "The fire brigade has nothing to do with liability for intentional arson."], ["The insurer pays half, because a fire started intentionally is also a covered event.", "There is no partial payment; loss caused intentionally is excluded from payment entirely."], ["The insurer must pay if the policy contains no intent exclusion.", "The law itself already excludes intent; no policy provision is needed for that."]], "1i.2"],
["A batch of foodstuffs in storage spoils because of the inherent nature of the product, without any outside influence. What does the BW provide about this if the policy says nothing?", [["Loss caused by inherent vice of the item is not compensated, unless otherwise agreed.", "Inherent vice is a property of the item that causes loss without an external cause; the law excludes that loss, but the parties can agree otherwise."], ["Loss caused by inherent vice is always compensated, because the item is insured.", "The law in fact assumes exclusion of inherent vice, unless the policy offers cover."], ["Loss caused by inherent vice may by law never be co-insured.", "The exclusion is non-mandatory law; the parties can agree cover for inherent vice."], ["Loss caused by inherent vice always falls under the liability of the supplier or producer.", "Whether the supplier is liable depends on the sales contract; it is separate from what the policy covers."]], "1i.2"],
["On Monday a business owner takes out a contents insurance, while he knows that his business premises were already flooded at the weekend. He then reports that loss to the insurer. What is correct?", [["No cover, because insurance requires uncertainty and the loss was already established.", "The law requires uncertainty about the occurrence of loss when the insurance is concluded; a loss that has already occurred and is known falls outside the insurance."], ["There is cover, because the loss was reported within the first week of the insurance.", "The time of reporting is not decisive; what matters is the absence of uncertainty when the insurance was concluded."], ["There is cover, but the insurer may apply a higher excess / deductible.", "A higher excess / deductible does not solve the lack of uncertainty; the loss falls entirely outside it."], ["There is cover, because the insurer asked no questions about earlier loss when concluding.", "The uncertainty requirement applies independently of the questionnaire; even without a question, an already known loss is not covered."]], "1g.3"],
["A bv has a commercial contents insurance that is tacitly renewed after the first year. Does the statutory rule that a consumer can terminate at any time with one month's notice after tacit renewal also apply to this bv?", [["No, that rule applies only to consumers; for the bv the policy agreements apply.", "The monthly right of termination after renewal protects policyholders who, as natural persons, are not acting in the course of a profession or business; for commercial policies the conditions determine when termination is possible."], ["Yes, that rule applies to every policyholder, commercial or private.", "The termination rule after tacit renewal is expressly limited to consumers."], ["Yes, but only if the bv has fewer than ten employees.", "The law makes no distinction by number of employees; the question is whether the policyholder is a consumer."], ["No, a commercial insurance can never be terminated during the term after renewal.", "Termination during the term is possible if the conditions allow it, for example after a loss or a premium change."]], "1a.1"],
["An employer wants to offer its employees a collective health insurance. What has applied since 2023 to the discount on the basic insurance through such a group contract?", [["There is no longer a group discount on the basic insurance; there can be on supplementary insurances.", "The possibility of a discount on the basic insurance through a group was abolished as of 2023; the benefits now lie mainly in supplementary packages and service."], ["The discount on the basic insurance is still a maximum of 10% of the premium.", "That maximum discount of 10% was previously reduced and abolished entirely as of 2023."], ["Only employers with more than a hundred employees may still give a discount on the basic insurance.", "The abolition applies to all groups, regardless of the employer's size."], ["With a group contract the employer is obliged to pay the full premium of the basic insurance.", "An employer pays the income-related contribution under the Health Insurance Act (Zvw), but not the nominal premium of the basic insurance."]], "1c.21"],
["An employer with both office staff and scaffolders wants one collective accident insurance for all its employees. Why can the premium differ per group of employees?", [["Because accident insurers generally divide occupations into risk classes by accident likelihood.", "Scaffolders run a higher accident risk at work than office staff; this translates into a higher risk class and premium."], ["Because the premium legally depends on the level of the salary of the employee concerned.", "The premium of an accident insurance mainly follows the sum insured and the occupational risk, not a statutory salary rule."], ["Because office staff may not be co-insured in a collective accident insurance for employees.", "Office staff can perfectly well be co-insured; the difference lies in the risk."], ["Because scaffolders are already insured against accidents through the WAM insurance of their employer.", "The WAM concerns liability in traffic with motor vehicles and does not cover industrial accidents."]], "1c.24"],
["After a fire, an adviser helps its commercial client to submit and substantiate the claim to the insurer. Does this fall under insurance distribution within the meaning of the IDD?", [["Yes, assistance in the performance of the insurance, such as with a claim, also falls under it.", "The IDD defines distribution broadly; besides advising and concluding, help with a claim is also included."], ["No, the IDD only concerns advice in advance and the moment the insurance itself is concluded.", "The definition continues after conclusion; the performance of the contract is also included."], ["No, claims handling always falls under the Wwft.", "The Wwft concerns money laundering and terrorist financing, not help with submitting a claim."], ["Only if the adviser sends a separate invoice for it.", "The way of remuneration does not determine whether the activity falls under the definition."]], "3e.1"],
["An adviser gets a new commercial client, a bv. What information does it as a rule mainly obtain from the Chamber of Commerce extract?", [["The legal form, the directors with their powers and the registered activities.", "The extract shows who may represent the company and what the business does; that is important for advice and for signing."], ["The company's claims history with the various insurers over the past few years.", "The claims history is not in the trade register; the adviser must request it from the client or insurers."], ["The employees' sickness absence and accidents in the recent years of the company.", "Such data are in the company's own records and the accident register, not in the trade register."], ["The current value of the contents, stock and business buildings of the company.", "Values appear from the asset records, the annual accounts or a valuation, not from the extract."]], "1d.1"],
["A wholesaler pays a premium of €2,000, including costs, for its contents insurance. How much insurance premium tax is as a rule added to that?", [["€420.00, because 21% insurance premium tax is due on the premium.", "On the premium of a non-life insurance such as a contents insurance, 21% insurance premium tax is levied: 21% of €2,000 is €420.00."], ["€0.00, because commercial non-life insurances are exempt from insurance premium tax.", "Commercial non-life insurances are generally not exempt; a contents insurance falls under the levy."], ["€180.00, because the reduced rate of 9% applies to business clients.", "A reduced rate of 9% does not exist for insurance premium tax; the rate is 21%."], ["€420.00, but the wholesaler can reclaim this as VAT.", "Insurance premium tax is not VAT and cannot be reclaimed as input tax."]], "1e.5"],
["A cash-in-transit and money insurance for a shop distinguishes a premises risk and a transit risk. Which situation falls under the transit risk?", [["An employee takes the day's takings to the bank and is robbed on the way.", "The transit risk covers money that is on the move, such as when paying in the day's takings at the bank."], ["During a burglary at night, the safe in the office is broken open.", "Money that is on the premises or in the safe falls under the premises risk."], ["A finance department employee transfers money to a fraudster.", "That is a form of fraud or deception via a payment, not transit of money."], ["During a robbery of the shop, the till drawer is emptied during the day.", "Money in the till in the shop falls under the premises risk, also in a robbery."]], "1e.10"],
["A four-year-old van is a total loss in an accident. At what value is the van compensated under most comprehensive policies?", [["The market value immediately before the accident, less the value of the remains.", "After the initial period with any new-for-old arrangement, comprehensive policies as a rule compensate the market value, minus the proceeds of the wreck."], ["The new value of a comparable new van, without deduction of remains.", "A new-for-old arrangement applies in most policies only in the first period after purchase, not after four years."], ["The original purchase price, because it is stated as the insured value on the policy.", "After four years the purchase price says little about the actual value; the market value is compensated."], ["The book value according to the depreciation in the company's annual accounts.", "The tax or commercial book value is not a measure of value in comprehensive policies."]], "1e.11"],
["A wholesaler insures its fluctuating stock on a declaration policy. How is the premium as a rule settled?", [["With a provisional premium, settled afterwards on the basis of the declared stock values.", "With a declaration policy the insured periodically declares the actual values; the final premium follows from that."], ["With a fixed annual premium on the highest stock of the year, without settlement.", "That is exactly what a declaration policy seeks to avoid; the premium follows the actual values."], ["Only afterwards, on the stock lost through a loss.", "The premium is calculated on the insured values, not only on the loss."], ["With a premium based on last year's turnover, which is never adjusted.", "With a declaration policy, settlement is in fact made afterwards on the declared values."]], "1e.13"],
["Why do non-life insurers and non-life intermediaries screen their commercial clients against sanctions lists, even if they do not fall under the Wwft?", [["The sanctions rules apply to everyone and financial firms must act with integrity.", "Whoever is not a Wwft institution must still comply with the sanctions prohibitions; screening is the way to demonstrate that."], ["Because the Wwft does apply to commercial non-life insurances above a certain annual premium.", "The Wwft does not designate non-life insurers and non-life intermediaries; the obligation follows from the sanctions legislation."], ["Because the Chamber of Commerce requires it for registration of the firm.", "The Chamber of Commerce (KvK) sets no screening requirements for financial service providers for registration."], ["Because the client otherwise may not pay the premium by direct debit.", "The payment method is separate from the sanctions duty; it concerns the prohibition on providing services to sanctioned persons."]], "1f.1"],
["An exporter asks for a transport insurance for a consignment to a country against which the EU has imposed trade restrictions. What must the adviser do?", [["Check whether the goods, destination or parties involved are subject to sanctions.", "Sanctions rules often prohibit not only the trade itself but also financial services, including insurance, that make that trade possible."], ["Simply place the insurance, because the adviser is only responsible for the insurance and not for the trade.", "Providing insurance may itself fall under the prohibition on providing services."], ["Place the insurance with a higher excess / deductible, so that the insurer runs less risk.", "A higher excess / deductible does not make a prohibited service permitted."], ["Only lower the sum insured to below the reporting threshold of the FIU (Financial Intelligence Unit).", "Sanctions rules have no lower limit below which providing prohibited services is permitted."]], "1f.1"],
["Planwerk BV in Eindhoven develops software that controls customers' production machines. The company has 25 employees and sells its software throughout Europe. Director Anouk has read about the new EU Product Liability Directive, which must be transposed into Dutch law by 9 December 2026 at the latest. She asks her adviser whether this has consequences for her company and her insurances. What is the best answer?", [["Yes, software then counts as a product; check whether the AVB covers product liability for software", "The new directive brings software and AI systems under product liability. For a software maker it is therefore important to check whether the AVB covers product liability for software."], ["Software never falls under product liability, because it is not a tangible product in the legal sense", "That was unclear under the old rules; the new directive in fact expressly provides that software is a product."], ["The directive applies only to producers established outside the EU and not to Dutch companies", "The directive applies to all producers that place products on the market in the EU, including Dutch ones."], ["The directive applies only if Planwerk intentionally supplies a defective product to its customers", "Product liability is strict liability: fault or intent of the producer is not a condition."]], "1g.3"]
];
